//! The file system, as an effect whose label names a root directory (the directory all paths are
//! relative to) that the run binds with `--fs NAME=PATH`.
//!
//! An unbound label is error `E0451`. A path that leaves its root is error `E0452`, and is rejected
//! before any operation runs. Such a path contains `..`, is absolute, or goes through a symlink
//! that leads outside the root. Different roots never conflict. `kind`, `exists`, `stat`, `scan`,
//! `remove`, `remove_tree`, `rename`, `symlink`, `link`, `read_link`, `lock`, `unlock` and `open`
//! with `ToCreate` act on a symlink itself. Every other operation follows it. `E0453` limits one
//! call, not a file: `read_file`, `read_at`, `read_chunk`, `walk` and `scan` reject a result above
//! 64 MiB, so read a larger file one range or chunk at a time, and a larger tree one directory at a
//! time. A path is text. A name on disk that is not valid UTF-8 is listed with U+FFFD in place of
//! its invalid bytes, and no operation can reach it.
//!
//! The host resolves a path's symlinks itself, following at most forty, and gives the operating
//! system the resulting path. So a symlink is confined to the root by where it leads, whether or
//! not anything exists there: a symlink that leads outside to a path that does not exist is
//! rejected like any other, and nothing is created there. An operation that acts on a symlink
//! itself is confined by the directory containing the symlink, and never looks where the symlink
//! leads. So a symlink that leads outside the root can still be read, renamed and removed. One gap
//! remains between the host's check and the operating system's own lookup: a program that can write
//! under the root can replace a directory in the path with a symlink, and the operation then goes
//! where that symlink leads. A symlink that replaces the file at the end of the path is rejected
//! rather than followed, except by `copy`, `set_mode` and `space`, which give the operating system
//! the path unchecked at that last step.
//!
//! `append` and `read_at` let a program use a file as a log rather than a single value. `sync` and
//! `lock` keep a cache with a separate index consistent: sync the data file before the index that
//! refers to it, and use `lock` to run a read-merge-write cycle exclusively across processes.
//! `open` returns a descriptor that reads or writes a file one chunk at a time until `close`. Every
//! operation on a descriptor is treated as a write to its root, so two tasks that use descriptors
//! under the same root conflict, as two writes to that root would. `std.io` opens a descriptor
//! under a `with_scope`, which closes it however its body ends, and reads and writes through the
//! scoped value.
//!
//! Use `write_atomic`, or `std.io`'s `replacing` for a sink, to write a file that must contain
//! either its old bytes or its new ones, never a mix. Each writes a temporary file next to the
//! target, syncs it, renames it over the target, and syncs the directory. `copy_tree` copies a tree
//! within a root, and `copy_tree_across` copies between two roots. `watch` and `await_changes`
//! report what changed under a directory by scanning it repeatedly. They ask the operating system
//! for nothing beyond what `scan` reads, so a change is detected on the first scan after it
//! happens, each scan reads every entry, and this works on any mounted file system. A function here
//! that cannot do what was requested raises `files.fail` with the path and the reason.
//!
//! The fake is `MemFs`, an in-memory file system with a `mem_` function for every operation.
//! `on_mem` runs code written against any root over one. `MemFs` follows symlinks and rejects paths
//! that leave its root as the host does, raising with `E0452`'s message. It enforces the owner
//! permission bits of a path (a new file is `rw-r--r--`, a new directory `rwxr-xr-x`), so a test
//! over it fails where the same code fails on a disk. It has no wall clock, so a lock in it never
//! expires. `on_mem_failing` runs code over a disk that stops accepting writes after a given number
//! of writes, and `mem_crashed` returns what a power loss would leave of a tree. Together they show
//! what a crash at each step of a write leaves behind. A test imports both `std.fs` and `std.fs
//! (fs)` to name the module and the effect.

import std.internal.native (found_int, found_text, native, wait, job_wait, observed, found, parts,
                            decode_int)
import std.laws (inverse)
import std.list
import std.path (segments)
import std.random (entropy, rand, token_hex, with_rand)
import std.time (as_millis, millis, nanos_at, time)

/// What a path refers to, read without following a symlink. `Missing` also covers what this run
/// cannot read.
pub type Kind = | File | Dir | Symlink | Missing

/// One entry a walk found: its path from the root, and its kind, with a symlink not followed.
pub type Entry = { path: String, kind: Kind }

/// A directory entry: its name, and its kind, where a symlink is reported as `Symlink` rather than
/// followed.
pub type Listed = { name: String, kind: Kind }

/// Returns the names in a directory listing, in the same order, or `None` when there is no listing.
pub fn listed_names(listing: Option<List<Listed>>) -> Option<List<String>> =
  match listing { Some(listed) -> Some(listed.map(|l: Listed| l.name)), None -> None }

/// Who may do what with a path: its owner, the owner's group, and everyone else.
pub type Mode = { owner: Access, group: Access, other: Access }

/// Whether a class of user may read, write and execute.
pub type Access = { read: Bool, write: Bool, execute: Bool }

/// How `open` opens a file: to read from its start, to overwrite its contents, to write after its
/// end, or to write a new file that must not exist yet.
pub type OpenMode = | ToRead | ToWrite | ToAppend | ToCreate

/// Why the file system could not do what was requested.
pub type FileError =
  /// Nothing is at the path, or a directory above it does not exist.
  | NotFound
  /// This run does not have permission.
  | Denied
  /// The path names a directory, or something else that is not a file.
  | NotAFile
  /// Something above the path is not a directory.
  | NotADirectory
  /// Something is already at the path.
  | Exists
  /// The file system is full, or this run's share of it is.
  | NoSpace
  /// A link would point out of the root it is under.
  | CrossesRoot
  /// More entries or more bytes than the caller's bound.
  | LimitExceeded
  /// Another reason from the host: its error number and message. The number is zero when the
  /// failed operation reports only that it failed, and the message then names the operation.
  | Other(Int, String)

/// The metadata of a path. A symlink is described as itself, not as the file it points to.
pub type Stat = {
  kind: Kind,
  /// A file's bytes; zero for a directory or a link.
  size: Int,
  /// When it was last modified, counted from the Unix epoch.
  modified: Instant,
  /// A link's mode reads `rwxrwxrwx`: it restricts nothing, and its target has its own bits.
  mode: Mode,
  /// How many names a file has; one for a directory or a link.
  links: Int,
  /// Which file system it is on.
  device: Int,
  /// Which file it is on that file system: two paths with the same `id` and `device` refer to one
  /// file, and a file keeps its `id` when it is renamed. What a removed file's `id` refers to later
  /// is unspecified.
  id: Int,
}

/// An entry found by a scan: its path from the root, and its `Stat`.
pub type ScanEntry = { path: String, stat: Stat }

/// The space in a file system: `total` bytes, `free` bytes that no file uses, and `available` bytes
/// this run may use, which is fewer than `free` when the system reserves some. Also the number of
/// files it can contain (`inodes`) and how many more it can take (`inodes_free`).
pub type Space = { total: Int, free: Int, available: Int, inodes: Int, inodes_free: Int }

/// The files under the root the label names.
pub nondet effect fs {
  /// A file's whole contents; `None` for anything that is not a readable file.
  read read_file[r](path: String) -> Option<Bytes>
  /// Returns up to `len` bytes starting at `offset`, or `None` when the path is not a readable
  /// file. The result is shorter than `len` when the range runs past the end of the file, and empty
  /// when `offset` is at or past the end. Compare the result's length with `len` to detect a short
  /// read. A negative offset or length raises `E0502`.
  read read_at[r](path: String, offset: Int, len: Int) -> Option<Bytes>
  /// Returns the entries of one directory, not its subtree, in byte order of name, each with its
  /// `Kind` as `kind` would report it. Returns `None` when the path is not a directory.
  read list_dir[r](path: String) -> Option<List<Listed>>
  /// What the path names, a symlink reported as `Symlink` rather than followed, so a walk that must not
  /// leave the root can reject one.
  read kind[r](path: String) -> Kind
  /// What the path resolves to, following a symlink to its target. `kind` reports the symlink
  /// itself; a caller that must not leave the root should use `kind`.
  read resolved_kind[r](path: String) -> Kind
  /// Whether anything is at the path, including a dangling link.
  read exists[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// A file's size in bytes.
  read file_size[r](path: String) -> Option<Int>
  /// When the path was last modified, in milliseconds since the Unix epoch.
  read modified_ms[r](path: String) -> Option<Int>
  /// Writes a whole file, replacing what was there.
  write write_file[r](path: String, body: Bytes) -> Bool
  /// Appends to a file, creating it if it does not exist, and returns the offset where the bytes
  /// were written. The time taken depends on the size of the new bytes, not on the size of the
  /// file. When two tasks append to the same file at once, each gets its own offset and the bytes
  /// do not overlap.
  write append[r](path: String, body: Bytes) -> Option<Int>
  /// Creates the directory and every missing ancestor; `true` if the directory already exists.
  write create_dir[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Removes one file, one symlink or one empty directory, never the root.
  write remove[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Moves a path within its root.
  write rename[r](from: String, to: String) -> Bool
  /// Makes what was written to the path durable. On a directory, it makes the names in it durable,
  /// which is what makes a `rename` itself durable. `false` when nothing is at the path.
  write sync[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Takes the lock file at `path`: `true` when this run created it, `false` when a holder still
  /// had it after two seconds of waiting. Locks are not reentrant. A lock file older than thirty
  /// seconds is treated as one whose holder died, and the next locker removes and takes it.
  write lock[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Releases a lock this run took; `false`, removing nothing, for one it does not have.
  write unlock[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Copies a file's bytes and permission bits over whatever file is at the destination; never a
  /// directory. A file copied onto itself, by this name or another of its names, is left unchanged.
  write copy[r](from: String, to: String) -> Bool
  /// Removes a directory with everything under it, never the root. A symlink in it is removed,
  /// not followed.
  write remove_tree[r](path: String) -> Bool
  /// Creates a new directory under `dir` whose name starts with `prefix`, and returns its path
  /// relative to the root.
  write temp_dir[r](dir: String, prefix: String) -> Option<String>
  /// Returns the absolute path of `path` on the real file system, with symlinks resolved. Use it
  /// when handing a path to another program, which does not know the root.
  read canonical[r](path: String) -> Option<String>
  /// Returns the permission bits (owner, group, other; read, write, execute) of `path`. Returns
  /// `None` when nothing is at the path.
  read mode[r](path: String) -> Option<Mode>
  /// Sets the nine permission bits, keeping the set-id and sticky bits.
  write set_mode[r](path: String, mode: Mode) -> Bool
  /// Makes `path` a link to `target`, which is resolved from the link's own directory.
  write symlink[r](path: String, target: String) -> Bool
  /// A link's target as written; `None` for anything but a link.
  read read_link[r](path: String) -> Option<String>
  /// Returns every entry below a directory, not the directory itself. Entries come in depth-first
  /// order, with the names in each directory sorted by byte. Each entry's path is relative to the
  /// root and begins with `path` as the caller wrote it. A symlink is listed as an entry and never
  /// followed, so a walk stays inside its root and cannot loop. Returns `None` when the path is not
  /// a directory.
  read walk[r](path: String) -> Option<List<Entry>>
  /// Sets the path's modification time, in milliseconds since the epoch. A negative time is error
  /// `E0502`.
  write set_modified[r](path: String, ms: Int) -> Bool
  /// Opens a file and returns the descriptor to read or write it through until `close`. No later
  /// `open` in this run returns the same descriptor. `ToRead` opens an existing file. `ToWrite`
  /// truncates the file, or creates it. `ToAppend` creates the file if it does not exist, and every
  /// write goes to the end of the file. `ToCreate` creates the file readable and writable by its
  /// owner only (`rw-------`) and fails with `Exists` if anything is at the path, including a link.
  /// So when two runs create the same path, one of them fails, and nothing is ever written through
  /// a link. A descriptor is valid only on the root it was opened on.
  write open[r](path: String, how: OpenMode) -> Result<Int, FileError>
  /// Reads the next bytes of a file opened `ToRead`, at most `max` of them, and advances the
  /// position past them. A result shorter than `max` does not mean the end of the file; an empty
  /// result does. It reads only the bytes it returns, never the whole file. Returns `None` when
  /// `file` is not open for reading on this root. A negative `max` is error `E0502`, and a `max`
  /// over the limit of a single read is `E0453`.
  write read_chunk[r](file: Int, max: Int) -> Option<Bytes>
  /// Writes all of `body` at the descriptor's position: after the last write, or at the end of the
  /// file when it was opened `ToAppend`. Nothing is buffered: the bytes are in the file when it
  /// returns, and `sync` of the path makes them durable. Returns `false` when `file` is not open
  /// for writing on this root, or when the write failed.
  write write_chunk[r](file: Int, body: Bytes) -> Bool
  /// Closes a descriptor. Returns `false` when `file` is not open on this root.
  write close[r](file: Int) -> Bool
  /// Returns the kind, size, modification time, permission bits, hard-link count (how many paths
  /// name the file) and identity (`device` and `id`, which tell whether two paths are the same
  /// file) of the path, reading them all in one call so they describe the same moment. A link is
  /// described as itself and never followed. Returns `None` when nothing is at the path, and for
  /// anything that is not a file, a directory or a link.
  read stat[r](path: String) -> Option<Stat>
  /// Every entry under a directory with what `stat` reads of it, in `walk`'s order: the whole tree
  /// when `deep`, the directory's own entries when not. A link is an entry and never followed.
  /// `None` when the path is not a directory.
  read scan[r](path: String, deep: Bool) -> Option<List<ScanEntry>>
  /// The space in the file system the path is on; `None` when nothing is at the path.
  read space[r](path: String) -> Option<Space>
  /// Makes `path` another name for the file at `to`, both under the root. They are one file, so
  /// what is written through either name is read through both, and the file exists until its last
  /// name is removed. Only a file can have a second name (otherwise `NotAFile`, including for a
  /// link, which is never followed), and nothing may be at `path` yet (otherwise `Exists`).
  write link[r](path: String, to: String) -> Result<Unit, FileError>
  /// Writes `before`, then `secret` in `encoding`, then `after`, as the whole contents of a file
  /// that only its owner may read or write (`rw-------`). An existing file gets that mode before it
  /// is truncated, so no other user can read any byte of the secret. A program uses this to store a
  /// generated key without ever holding the key as an ordinary, unprotected value. `encoding` is
  /// `"raw"` (the bytes themselves), `"hex"`, `"base64"`, `"base64url"` or `"base32"`, written as
  /// `Secret::encode` writes them; any other value is a runtime error. Returns `false` if the file
  /// could not be written.
  write write_secret[r](path: String, before: Bytes, secret: Secret<Bytes>, encoding: String, after: Bytes) -> Bool
  /// Returns the secret stored in a file between `before` and `after`, decoded from `encoding`,
  /// without exposing it as an ordinary value. It reads what `write_secret` wrote when given the
  /// same three arguments. Returns `None` when the file cannot be read (as for `read_file`), does
  /// not start with `before` and end with `after`, or has no valid `encoding` text between them. A
  /// file over 64 MiB is error `E0453`.
  read read_secret[r](path: String, before: Bytes, encoding: String, after: Bytes) -> Option<Secret<Bytes>>
}

/// What the functions of this module raise.
pub effect files {
  /// The file system could not do what a function requested: the path where it stopped, and why.
  raise fail(path: String, reason: FileError)
}

/// The mode as the nine letters `ls -l` writes, such as `rwxr-xr-x`.
pub fn mode_text(m: Mode) -> String =
  access_text(m.owner) ++ access_text(m.group) ++ access_text(m.other)

fn access_text(a: Access) -> String =
  (if a.read { "r" } else { "-" }) ++ (if a.write { "w" } else { "-" })
    ++ (if a.execute { "x" } else { "-" })

/// Parses the nine-letter text that `mode_text` writes, such as `rwxr-xr-x`, and returns the mode.
/// Returns `None` for anything that is not exactly nine of those letters and dashes.
pub fn mode_of_text(text: String) -> Option<Mode> / {abort.raise} = {
  let b = Bytes::of_string(text);
  if b.len() != 9 { None }
  else { Some({ owner: access_at(b, 0)?, group: access_at(b, 3)?, other: access_at(b, 6)? }) }
}

fn access_at(b: Bytes, at: Int) -> Option<Access> / {abort.raise} = {
  let read = flag(b, at, 114)?;
  let write = flag(b, at + 1, 119)?;
  let execute = flag(b, at + 2, 120)?;
  Some({ read: read, write: write, execute: execute })
}

fn flag(b: Bytes, i: Int, letter: Int) -> Option<Bool> / {abort.raise} =
  if b.at(i) == letter { Some(true) } else if b.at(i) == 45 { Some(false) } else { None }

/// The mode as its nine permission bits, the owner's highest: `rwxr-xr-x` is octal 755.
pub fn mode_bits(m: Mode) -> Int =
  access_bits(m.owner) * 64 + access_bits(m.group) * 8 + access_bits(m.other)

fn access_bits(a: Access) -> Int =
  (if a.read { 4 } else { 0 }) + (if a.write { 2 } else { 0 }) + (if a.execute { 1 } else { 0 })

/// The mode that nine permission bits represent. Bits above those nine are ignored.
pub fn mode_of_bits(bits: Int) -> Mode = {
  let of = |access_at: Int| access(access_at & 4 != 0, access_at & 2 != 0, access_at & 1 != 0);
  { owner: of(bits / 64 & 7), group: of(bits / 8 & 7), other: of(bits & 7) }
}

host for fs by host_handler

fn host_handler<a | e>(
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[*], fs.write[*] | e},
) -> a / {native.read, wait.read, abort.raise | e} =
  handle {
    go()
  } with {
    fs.read_file[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 0, found(job_wait(fs_read_file(l, path)))),
    fs.read_at[*l](path, offset, len) -> if offset < 0 || len < 0 {
      panic(
        "`fs.read_at` was given offset " ++ offset.to_string() ++ " and length " ++ len.to_string(),
      )
    } else { noted(l, path, 0, found(job_wait(fs_read_at(l, path, offset, len)))) },
    fs.list_dir[*l](path) ->
      noted(l, path, 1, match found(job_wait(fs_list_dir(l, path))) {
        Some(flat) -> Some(parts(flat).map(|entry: Bytes| listed_of(entry))),
        None -> None,
      }),
    fs.kind[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 2, kind_of(job_wait(fs_kind(l, path)))),
    fs.resolved_kind[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 2, kind_of(job_wait(fs_resolved(l, path)))),
    fs.exists[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 2, succeeded(job_wait(fs_exists(l, path)))),
    fs.file_size[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 0, found_int(job_wait(fs_file_size(l, path)))),
    fs.modified_ms[*l](path) -> found_int(job_wait(fs_modified_ms(l, path))),
    fs.write_file[*l](path, body) ->
      noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_write_file(l, path, body)))),
    fs.append[*l](path, body) -> noted(l, path, 4, found_int(job_wait(fs_append(l, path, body)))),
    fs.create_dir[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_create_dir(l, path)))),
    fs.remove[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_remove(l, path)))),
    fs.rename[*l](from, to) -> noted(l, from, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_rename(l, from, to)))),
    fs.sync[*l](path) -> succeeded(job_wait(fs_sync(l, path))),
    fs.lock[*l](path) -> succeeded(job_wait(fs_lock(l, path))),
    fs.unlock[*l](path) -> succeeded(job_wait(fs_unlock(l, path))),
    fs.copy[*l](from, to) -> noted(l, from, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_copy(l, from, to)))),
    fs.remove_tree[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_remove_tree(l, path)))),
    fs.temp_dir[*l](dir, prefix) -> match found_text(job_wait(fs_temp_dir(l, dir, prefix))) {
      Some(made) -> noted(l, made, 4, Some(made)),
      None -> None,
    },
    fs.canonical[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 2, found_text(job_wait(fs_canonical(l, path)))),
    fs.mode[*l](path) ->
      noted(l, path, 0, match found(job_wait(fs_mode(l, path))) {
        Some(bits) -> Some(mode_of_bits(decode_int(bits))),
        None -> None,
      }),
    fs.set_mode[*l](path, mode) ->
      noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_set_mode(l, path, mode_bits(mode))))),
    fs.symlink[*l](path, target) ->
      noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_symlink(l, path, target)))),
    fs.read_link[*l](path) -> noted(l, path, 0, found_text(job_wait(fs_read_link(l, path)))),
    fs.walk[*l](path) ->
      noted(l, path, 3, match found(job_wait(fs_walk(l, path))) {
        Some(flat) -> Some(parts(flat).map(|entry: Bytes| entry_of(entry))),
        None -> None,
      }),
    fs.set_modified[*l](path, ms) -> if ms < 0 {
      panic("`fs.set_modified` was given " ++ ms.to_string() ++ " milliseconds")
    } else { noted(l, path, 4, succeeded(job_wait(fs_set_modified(l, path, ms)))) },
    fs.open[*l](path, how) -> {
      let reply = job_wait(fs_open(l, path, open_mode_number(how)));
      observed(l, path, match how { ToRead -> 0, _ -> 4 });
      let rest = reply.slice(1, reply.len());
      if reply.at(0) == 1 { Ok(decode_int(rest)) } else { Err(file_error_of(rest)) }
    },
    fs.read_chunk[*l](file, max) -> if max < 0 {
      panic("`fs.read_chunk` was asked for " ++ max.to_string() ++ " bytes")
    } else { found(job_wait(fs_read_chunk(l, file, max))) },
    fs.write_chunk[*l](file, body) -> succeeded(job_wait(fs_write_chunk(l, file, body))),
    fs.close[*l](file) -> succeeded(job_wait(fs_close(l, file))),
    fs.stat[*l](path) ->
      noted(
        l,
        path,
        2,
        noted(l, path, 0, match found(job_wait(fs_stat(l, path))) {
          Some(flat) -> Some(stat_of(flat)),
          None -> None,
        }),
      ),
    fs.scan[*l](path, deep) ->
      noted(l, path, 3, match found(job_wait(fs_scan(l, path, deep))) {
        Some(flat) -> Some(parts(flat).map(|seen: Bytes| scan_entry_of(seen))),
        None -> None,
      }),
    fs.space[*l](path) -> match found(job_wait(fs_space(l, path))) {
      Some(flat) -> Some(space_of(flat)),
      None -> None,
    },
    fs.link[*l](path, to) -> {
      let reply = job_wait(fs_link(l, path, to));
      observed(l, path, 4);
      if reply.at(0) == 1 { Ok(()) } else { Err(file_error_of(reply.slice(1, reply.len()))) }
    },
    fs.write_secret[*l](path, before, secret, encoding, after) ->
      noted(
        l,
        path,
        4,
        succeeded(job_wait(fs_write_secret(l, path, before, secret, encoding, after))),
      ),
    fs.read_secret[*l](path, before, encoding, after) ->
      noted(l, path, 0, match found(job_wait(fs_read_secret(l, path, before, encoding, after))) {
        Some(number) -> Some(fs_secret_taken(decode_int(number))),
        None -> None,
      }),
  }

fn noted<b>(l: String, path: String, kind: Int, result: b) -> b / {native.read} = {
  observed(l, path, kind);
  result
}

fn succeeded(reply: Bytes) -> Bool / {abort.raise} = reply.at(0) == 1

fn kind_of(reply: Bytes) -> Kind / {abort.raise} =
  match reply.at(0) { 0 -> File, 1 -> Dir, 2 -> Symlink, _ -> Missing }

fn open_mode_number(how: OpenMode) -> Int =
  match how { ToRead -> 0, ToWrite -> 1, ToAppend -> 2, ToCreate -> 3 }

fn file_error_of(flat: Bytes) -> FileError / {abort.raise} =
  match flat.at(0) {
    0 -> NotFound,
    1 -> Denied,
    2 -> NotAFile,
    3 -> NotADirectory,
    4 -> Exists,
    5 -> NoSpace,
    _ -> match parts(flat.slice(1, flat.len())) {
      [code, words] -> Other(decode_int(code), String::of_bytes_lossy(words)),
      _ -> panic("the file system returned an error that is not a code and a message"),
    },
  }

fn listed_of(flat: Bytes) -> Listed / {abort.raise} =
  match parts(flat) {
    [name, kind] -> { name: String::of_bytes_lossy(name), kind: kind_of(kind) },
    _ -> panic("the file system returned a directory entry that is not a name and a kind"),
  }

fn entry_of(flat: Bytes) -> Entry / {abort.raise} = {
  let read = listed_of(flat);
  { path: read.name, kind: read.kind }
}

fn stat_of(flat: Bytes) -> Stat / {abort.raise} =
  match parts(flat) {
    [kind, size, modified, mode, links, device, id] ->
      {
        kind: kind_of(kind),
        size: decode_int(size),
        modified: Instant(decode_int(modified)),
        mode: mode_of_bits(decode_int(mode)),
        links: decode_int(links),
        device: decode_int(device),
        id: decode_int(id),
      },
    _ -> panic("the file system returned file information with the wrong number of fields"),
  }

fn scan_entry_of(flat: Bytes) -> ScanEntry / {abort.raise} =
  match parts(flat) {
    [path, stat] -> { path: String::of_bytes_lossy(path), stat: stat_of(stat) },
    _ ->
      panic(
        "the file system returned a directory scan result that is not a path followed by file information",
      ),
  }

fn space_of(flat: Bytes) -> Space / {abort.raise} =
  match parts(flat) {
    [total, free, available, inodes, inodes_free] ->
      {
        total: decode_int(total),
        free: decode_int(free),
        available: decode_int(available),
        inodes: decode_int(inodes),
        inodes_free: decode_int(inodes_free),
      },
    _ -> panic("the file system returned space information with the wrong number of fields"),
  }

extern fn fs_read_file(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_read_at(label: String, path: String, offset: Int, len: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_list_dir(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_kind(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_resolved(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_exists(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_file_size(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_modified_ms(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_write_file(label: String, path: String, body: Bytes) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_append(label: String, path: String, body: Bytes) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_create_dir(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_remove(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_rename(label: String, from: String, to: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_sync(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_lock(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_unlock(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_copy(label: String, from: String, to: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_remove_tree(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_temp_dir(label: String, dir: String, prefix: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_canonical(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_mode(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_set_mode(label: String, path: String, bits: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_symlink(label: String, path: String, target: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_read_link(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_walk(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_set_modified(label: String, path: String, ms: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_open(label: String, path: String, how: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_read_chunk(label: String, file: Int, max: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_write_chunk(label: String, file: Int, body: Bytes) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_close(label: String, file: Int) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_stat(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_scan(label: String, path: String, deep: Bool) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_space(label: String, path: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_link(label: String, path: String, to: String) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_write_secret(
  label: String,
  path: String,
  before: Bytes,
  secret: Secret<Bytes>,
  encoding: String,
  after: Bytes,
) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_read_secret(
  label: String,
  path: String,
  before: Bytes,
  encoding: String,
  after: Bytes,
) -> Int / {native.read}

extern fn fs_secret_taken(number: Int) -> Secret<Bytes> / {native.read}

/// An in-memory directory tree, a value that stands in for the real file system. A link maps to its
/// target as written. A path with no entry in `modes` has the mode a new file or directory gets.
/// `opened` records each descriptor that is not yet closed. `short` is the most bytes one
/// `read_chunk` returns, or zero for no limit. `ids` gives each path the id of the file, directory
/// or link it refers to, and `next_id` is the next unused id. `shared` maps the id of each file
/// that has more than one path (a hard link) to the number of paths. `space` is the value
/// `fs.space` returns. `synced_bodies` stores each file's bytes as of its last sync. `synced_names`
/// maps each path to the id of the file that was at that path when its directory was last synced.
/// `mem_crashed` uses both to decide what survives a power loss.
pub type MemFs = {
  files: Map<String, Body>,
  dirs: Map<String, Int>,
  links: Map<String, String>,
  modes: Map<String, Mode>,
  modified: Map<String, Int>,
  locks: Map<String, Int>,
  opened: Map<Int, MemOpenFile>,
  short: Int,
  clock: Int,
  ids: Map<String, Int>,
  next_id: Int,
  shared: Map<Int, Int>,
  space: Space,
  synced_bodies: Map<Int, Body>,
  synced_names: Map<String, Int>,
}

/// The contents of a file in a `MemFs`: plain bytes, or a secret written by `fs.write_secret`,
/// stored as given. Ply code cannot read a secret's bytes, so `mem_read`, `read_at` and
/// `read_chunk` treat a secret file as unreadable. `fs.read_secret` returns the secret only when
/// given the same `before`, `encoding` and `after` it was written with. `mem_framed_secret` returns
/// the stored value.
pub type Body = | Plain(Bytes) | Sealed(FramedSecret)

/// A secret as `fs.write_secret` writes one: `before`, then `secret` in `encoding`, then `after`.
pub type FramedSecret = { before: Bytes, secret: Secret<Bytes>, encoding: String, after: Bytes }

fn check_encoding(encoding: String) -> Unit / {abort.raise} =
  if encoding == "raw" { () } else {
    let _ = Secret::encode(encoding, Secret::of_bytes(b""));
    ()
  }

fn encoded_size(n: Int, encoding: String) -> Int =
  if encoding == "hex" { 2 * n }
  else if encoding == "base64" { (n + 2) / 3 * 4 }
  else if encoding == "base64url" { (4 * n + 2) / 3 }
  else if encoding == "base32" { (n + 4) / 5 * 8 }
  else { n }

fn body_size(body: Body) -> Int =
  match body {
    Plain(bytes) -> bytes.len(),
    Sealed(f) -> f.before.len() + encoded_size(f.secret.len(), f.encoding) + f.after.len(),
  }

fn appended(body: Body, more: Bytes) -> Body =
  match body {
    Plain(bytes) -> Plain(bytes.concat(more)),
    Sealed(f) -> Sealed({ ..f, after: f.after.concat(more) }),
  }

fn secret_between(
  bytes: Bytes,
  before: Bytes,
  encoding: String,
  after: Bytes,
) -> Option<Secret<Bytes>> / {abort.raise} = {
  let size = bytes.len();
  let inner = before.len();
  let outer = size - after.len();
  if size < inner + after.len() || bytes.slice(0, inner) != before
      || bytes.slice(outer, size) != after { None }
  else if encoding == "raw" { Some(Secret::of_bytes(bytes.slice(inner, outer))) } else {
    Secret::decode(encoding, Secret::of_string(String::of_bytes_lossy(bytes.slice(inner, outer))))
  }
}

/// A file open in a `MemFs`: the path it was opened at after following links, the open mode, and
/// the descriptor's current offset in the file.
pub type MemOpenFile = { key: String, how: OpenMode, at: Int }

/// An open's two results: the tree it produced, and the descriptor or why there is none.
pub type OpenResult = { fs: MemFs, file: Result<Int, FileError> }

/// A chunk read's two results: the tree it produced, and the bytes.
pub type ReadChunkResult = { fs: MemFs, bytes: Option<Bytes> }

/// A write's two results: the tree it produced and whether it happened.
pub type WriteResult = { fs: MemFs, ok: Bool }

/// An append's two results: the tree it produced and the offset where the bytes were written.
pub type AppendResult = { fs: MemFs, at: Option<Int> }

/// A temporary directory's two results: the tree it produced and the path it created.
pub type TempDirResult = { fs: MemFs, path: Option<String> }

/// A hard link's two results: the tree it produced, and whether it was made or why not.
pub type LinkResult = { fs: MemFs, done: Result<Unit, FileError> }

/// A tree with only its root, in a one-tebibyte file system with half of it free.
pub fn mem_empty() -> MemFs =
  {
    files: Map::new(),
    dirs: Map::new(),
    links: Map::new(),
    modes: Map::new(),
    modified: Map::new(),
    locks: Map::new(),
    opened: Map::new(),
    short: 0,
    clock: 1,
    ids: Map::new(),
    next_id: 1,
    shared: Map::new(),
    space: {
      total: 0x100_0000_0000,
      free: 0x80_0000_0000,
      available: 0x80_0000_0000,
      inodes: 0x10_0000,
      inodes_free: 0x8_0000,
    },
    synced_bodies: Map::new(),
    synced_names: Map::new(),
  }

/// The tree in a file system with this much space, which is what `space` returns for it.
pub fn mem_with_space(tree: MemFs, room: Space) -> MemFs = { ..tree, space: room }

/// A tree seeded with files, every parent directory created along the way.
pub fn mem_of(files: List<{ path: String, body: Bytes }>) -> MemFs / {abort.raise} =
  List::fold(files, mem_empty(), |tree: MemFs, entry: { path: String, body: Bytes }|
    mem_write(mem_create_dir(tree, parent_of(entry.path)).fs, entry.path, entry.body).fs)

/// Returns whether `path` is relative and has no `..` segment, which is the part of the real file
/// system's check that looks only at the path's text. It does not check links.
pub fn mem_confined(path: String) -> Bool =
  !path.starts_with("/") && path.split("/").filter(|s: String| s == "..").len() == 0

/// Returns what `fs.read_file` returns on the tree: the file's bytes, or `None` when the file is
/// missing or unreadable, or was written by `fs.write_secret`.
pub fn mem_read(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Bytes> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  match tree.files.get(key) {
    Some(Plain(bytes)) -> if readable(tree, key) { Some(bytes) } else { None },
    _ -> None,
  }
}

/// The contents of a file `fs.write_secret` wrote; `None` for any other path.
pub fn mem_framed_secret(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<FramedSecret> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  match tree.files.get(key) { Some(Sealed(f)) -> Some(f), _ -> None }
}

/// Returns what `fs.read_secret` returns on the tree. For a plain file, it is the secret between
/// `before` and `after`. For a file written by `fs.write_secret`, it is that file's secret, and
/// only when `before`, `encoding` and `after` equal the ones it was written with.
pub fn mem_read_secret(
  tree: MemFs,
  path: String,
  before: Bytes,
  encoding: String,
  after: Bytes,
) -> Option<Secret<Bytes>> / {abort.raise} = {
  check_encoding(encoding);
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if !readable(tree, key) { None } else {
    match tree.files.get(key) {
      Some(Sealed(f)) -> if f.before == before && f.encoding == encoding && f.after == after {
        Some(f.secret)
      } else { None },
      Some(Plain(bytes)) -> secret_between(bytes, before, encoding, after),
      None -> None,
    }
  }
}

/// Returns whether anything exists at `path`. A link at the last segment is not followed, so a link
/// to a missing target exists.
pub fn mem_exists(f: MemFs, path: String) -> Bool / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(f, path, false) { Some(key) -> kind_at(f, key) != Missing, None -> false }

/// Returns a file's size in bytes, or `None` when there is no such file. Reading the size needs no
/// read permission.
pub fn mem_size(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Int> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  let body = tree.files.get(key)?;
  Some(body_size(body))
}

/// The bytes in the range, which may be fewer than requested: a file can end before the range
/// does, and a caller that needs all of it compares the result's length with the one it requested.
pub fn mem_read_at(
  tree: MemFs,
  path: String,
  offset: Int,
  len: Int,
) -> Option<Bytes> / {abort.raise} = {
  let body = mem_read(tree, path)?;
  let size = body.len();
  let start = at_most(offset, size);
  Some(body.slice(start, at_most(start + len, size)))
}

fn at_most(n: Int, hi: Int) -> Int = if n > hi { hi } else { n }

/// Returns when the path was last modified: for a file, the time of its last write, and for a
/// directory, the time it was created.
pub fn mem_modified(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Int> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  match tree.modified.get(key) { Some(at) -> Some(at), None -> tree.dirs.get(key) }
}

/// What `fs.resolved_kind` returns over the tree. A chain of more than forty links counts as a loop
/// and resolves to nothing.
pub fn mem_resolved_kind(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Kind / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> match kind_at(tree, key) { Symlink -> Missing, k -> k },
    None -> Missing,
  }

/// Returns what `fs.kind` returns on the tree: the kind of the entry at `path`, without following a
/// link at the last segment, or `Missing`.
pub fn mem_kind(f: MemFs, path: String) -> Kind / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(f, path, false) { Some(key) -> kind_at(f, key), None -> Missing }

/// Returns the entries directly in a directory, not those below it, sorted by name in ascending
/// order so the result depends only on the directory's contents.
pub fn mem_list(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<List<Listed>> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) != Dir || !readable(tree, key) { None } else {
    Some(
      names_in(tree, key).map(|name: String| { name: name, kind: kind_at(tree, under(key, name)) }),
    )
  }
}

/// Depth first, each directory's names ascending, a link listed and never followed.
pub fn mem_walk(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<List<Entry>> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) != Dir { None }
  else { Some(walk_from(tree, key, normalize(path), [], tree.dirs.len())) }
}

fn walk_from(
  tree: MemFs,
  key: String,
  named: String,
  accumulator: List<Entry>,
  room: Int,
) -> List<Entry> / {abort.raise} =
  if !readable(tree, key) { accumulator } else {
    names_in(tree, key).fold(accumulator, |out: List<Entry>, name: String| {
      let child = under(key, name);
      let shown = under(named, name);
      let kind = kind_at(tree, child);
      let listed = out.push({ path: shown, kind: kind });
      if kind != Dir { listed }
      else if room <= 0 { panic("a directory contains itself through a link or a cycle") }
      else { walk_from(tree, child, shown, listed, room - 1) }
    })
  }

/// Returns the tree after writing `body` to the file at `path`, as `fs.write_file` does, and
/// whether that happened. `ok` is false, with the tree unchanged, when the parent directory is
/// missing or not writable, or a directory is at the path.
pub fn mem_write(tree: MemFs, path: String, body: Bytes) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> if can_write_file(tree, key) { { fs: stored(tree, key, Plain(body)), ok: true } }
    else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Returns the tree after writing the secret in `framed` to the file at `path`, as
/// `fs.write_secret` does. `framed` holds the bytes before the secret, the secret, its encoding and
/// the bytes after it. The file is stored as a secret that only its owner can read or write. `ok`
/// is false in the cases where `mem_write` returns false.
pub fn mem_write_secret(
  tree: MemFs,
  path: String,
  framed: FramedSecret,
) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} = {
  check_encoding(framed.encoding);
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> if can_write_file(tree, key) {
      { fs: with_mode(stored(tree, key, Sealed(framed)), key, owner_only()), ok: true }
    } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }
}

/// Returns the tree after appending `body` to the file at `path`, as `fs.append` does, and the
/// offset where `body` was written. The file is created if it is missing, so the first append to a
/// new log needs no earlier write. The offset is `None` in the cases where `mem_write` fails.
pub fn mem_append(tree: MemFs, path: String, body: Bytes) -> AppendResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> if can_write_file(tree, key) {
      let had = match tree.files.get(key) { Some(b) -> b, None -> Plain(b"") };
      { fs: stored(tree, key, appended(had, body)), at: Some(body_size(had)) }
    } else { { fs: tree, at: None } },
    None -> { fs: tree, at: None },
  }

/// Copies the bytes and mode of the file at `from` over the file at `to`, and never replaces a
/// directory. Succeeds without changing anything when both paths are names of the same file, such
/// as hard links to it.
pub fn mem_copy(tree: MemFs, from: String, to: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, from, true) {
    Some(source) -> match key_of(tree, to, true) {
      Some(destination) -> match tree.files.get(source) {
        Some(body) -> if source == destination || one_file(tree, source, destination) {
          { fs: tree, ok: true }
        } else if readable(tree, source) && can_write_file(tree, destination) {
          {
            fs: with_mode(stored(tree, destination, body), destination, mode_at(tree, source)),
            ok: true,
          }
        } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
        None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
      },
      None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Returns the tree after `fs.sync` on `path`. For a file, its current bytes become the bytes that
/// survive a power loss. For a directory, the files now in it become the files that survive a power
/// loss there. `ok` is false, with the tree unchanged, when `path` is neither a file nor a
/// directory.
pub fn mem_sync(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> match kind_at(tree, key) {
      File -> { fs: sync_file(with_id(tree, key), key), ok: true },
      Dir -> { fs: sync_dir(tree, key), ok: true },
      _ -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

fn sync_file(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> MemFs =
  match tree.ids.get(key) {
    Some(id) -> match tree.files.get(key) {
      Some(body) -> { ..tree, synced_bodies: tree.synced_bodies.insert(id, body) },
      None -> tree,
    },
    None -> tree,
  }

fn sync_dir(tree: MemFs, dir: String) -> MemFs = {
  let left = tree.synced_names.keys().fold(tree.synced_names, |m: Map<String, Int>, key: String|
    if parent_of(key) == dir && !tree.files.contains(key) { m.remove(key) } else { m });
  {
    ..tree,
    synced_names: tree.files.fold(left, |m: Map<String, Int>, key: String, _body: Body|
      if parent_of(key) != dir { m }
      else { match tree.ids.get(key) { Some(id) -> m.insert(key, id), None -> m } }),
  }
}

/// The tree with everything in it synced: what a test starts from when its files should count as
/// having been on disk all along.
pub fn mem_sync_all(tree: MemFs) -> MemFs =
  tree.files.fold(
    { ..tree, synced_bodies: Map::new(), synced_names: Map::new() },
    |t: MemFs, key: String, body: Body| {
      let named = with_id(t, key);
      match named.ids.get(key) {
        Some(id) ->
          {
            ..named,
            synced_bodies: named.synced_bodies.insert(id, body),
            synced_names: named.synced_names.insert(key, id),
          },
        None -> named,
      }
    },
  )

/// Returns what a power loss leaves of the tree. A file survives if its directory was synced while
/// the directory contained the file, even if the file was removed or renamed away since. A
/// surviving file contains the bytes it had when the file itself was last synced, or no bytes if
/// the file was never synced. A file that no synced directory listed is gone. Directories and links
/// are unchanged, and no descriptor or lock survives. In the returned tree everything counts as
/// synced, so a second crash changes nothing.
pub fn mem_crashed(tree: MemFs) -> MemFs = {
  let surviving_names = tree.synced_names.fold(
    Map::new(),
    |m: Map<String, Int>, key: String, id: Int|
      if kind_at(tree, parent_of(key)) != Dir || tree.dirs.contains(key)
          || tree.links.contains(key) { m }
      else { m.insert(key, id) },
  );
  let survives = |key: String|
    surviving_names.contains(key) || tree.dirs.contains(key) || tree.links.contains(key);
  let counted = surviving_names.fold(Map::new(), |n: Map<Int, Int>, _key: String, id: Int|
    n.insert(id, match n.get(id) { Some(names) -> names + 1, None -> 1 }));
  mem_sync_all({
    ..tree,
    files: surviving_names.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, Body>, key: String, id: Int|
      m.insert(key, match tree.synced_bodies.get(id) { Some(body) -> body, None -> Plain(b"") })),
    ids: tree.ids.fold(surviving_names, |m: Map<String, Int>, key: String, id: Int|
      if tree.files.contains(key) || m.contains(key) { m } else { m.insert(key, id) }),
    shared: counted.fold(Map::new(), |n: Map<Int, Int>, id: Int, names: Int|
      if names > 1 { n.insert(id, names) } else { n }),
    modes: tree.modes.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, Mode>, key: String, mode: Mode|
      if survives(key) { m.insert(key, mode) } else { m }),
    modified: tree.modified.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, Int>, key: String, at: Int|
      if survives(key) { m.insert(key, at) } else { m }),
    locks: Map::new(),
    opened: Map::new(),
  })
}

/// Creates the directory and every missing ancestor, as the host's `create_dir` does. Nothing is
/// created if any of them cannot be.
pub fn mem_create_dir(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> {
      let made = ancestors(key).fold({ fs: tree, ok: true }, |w: WriteResult, dir: String|
        if !w.ok { w } else {
          match kind_at(w.fs, dir) {
            Dir -> w,
            Missing -> if can_create(w.fs, dir) {
              {
                fs: with_id(
                  { ..w.fs, dirs: w.fs.dirs.insert(dir, w.fs.clock), clock: w.fs.clock + 1 },
                  dir,
                ),
                ok: true,
              }
            } else { { fs: w.fs, ok: false } },
            _ -> { fs: w.fs, ok: false },
          }
        });
      if made.ok { made } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } }
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Named from `prefix` and the tree's clock, so a test can predict its name.
pub fn mem_temp_dir(tree: MemFs, dir: String, prefix: String) -> TempDirResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, dir, true) {
    Some(key) -> if prefix.contains("/") || kind_at(tree, key) != Dir || !writable(tree, key) {
      { fs: tree, path: None }
    } else {
      let free = iterate(tree.clock, all_keys(tree).len() + 2, |c: Int|
        if kind_at(tree, under(key, prefix ++ c.to_string())) == Missing { Stop(c) }
        else { Continue(c + 1) });
      let name = prefix ++ free.to_string();
      let made = mem_create_dir({ ..tree, clock: free }, under(key, name));
      if made.ok { { fs: made.fs, path: Some(under(normalize(dir), name)) } }
      else { { fs: tree, path: None } }
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, path: None },
  }

/// Removes one file, one link, or one empty directory.
pub fn mem_remove(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, false) {
    Some(key) -> if key == "" || !writable(tree, parent_of(key)) { { fs: tree, ok: false } } else {
      match kind_at(tree, key) {
        Dir -> if names_in(tree, key).len() > 0 { { fs: tree, ok: false } }
        else { { fs: dropped(tree, key), ok: true } },
        Missing -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
        _ -> { fs: dropped(tree, key), ok: true },
      }
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Removes a link itself, or a directory with everything under it; never the root.
pub fn mem_remove_tree(f: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(f, path, false) {
    Some(key) -> if key == "" || kind_at(f, key) == Missing || !writable(f, parent_of(key)) {
      { fs: f, ok: false }
    } else {
      {
        fs: all_keys(f).fold(f, |t: MemFs, k: String|
          if k == key || k.starts_with(key ++ "/") { dropped(t, k) } else { t }),
        ok: true,
      }
    },
    None -> { fs: f, ok: false },
  }

/// Renames `from` to `to` within the tree, which is what makes a cache write atomic. A file or link
/// can replace an existing file or link, and a directory can move only to a path that does not
/// exist and never into itself. The moved entry keeps its modification time. If `from` and `to` are
/// two hard-link names of the same file, nothing changes and the call succeeds.
pub fn mem_rename(tree: MemFs, from: String, to: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, from, false) {
    Some(source) -> match key_of(tree, to, false) {
      Some(destination) -> if kind_at(tree, source) != Missing
          && (source == destination || one_file(tree, source, destination)) {
        { fs: tree, ok: true }
      } else if movable(tree, source, destination) {
        { fs: moved(tree, source, destination), ok: true }
      } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
      None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

fn one_file(tree: MemFs, a: String, b: String) -> Bool =
  tree.files.contains(a) && tree.files.contains(b) && tree.ids.get(a) != None
    && tree.ids.get(a) == tree.ids.get(b)

fn movable(tree: MemFs, source: String, destination: String) -> Bool = {
  let from = kind_at(tree, source);
  let to = kind_at(tree, destination);
  source != "" && destination != "" && from != Missing
    && kind_at(tree, parent_of(destination)) == Dir
    && writable(tree, parent_of(source))
    && writable(tree, parent_of(destination))
    && !String::starts_with(destination ++ "/", source ++ "/")
    && (to == Missing || (from != Dir && to != Dir))
}

fn moved(f: MemFs, source: String, destination: String) -> MemFs / {abort.raise} = {
  let cleared = if kind_at(f, destination) == Missing { f } else { dropped(f, destination) };
  let under_destination = |k: String| destination ++ k.slice(source.len(), k.len());
  let keyed = all_keys(cleared).fold(cleared, |t: MemFs, k: String|
    if k == source || k.starts_with(source ++ "/") { rekeyed(t, k, under_destination(k)) }
    else { t });
  let durable = if kind_at(f, source) != Dir { keyed.synced_names } else {
    keyed.synced_names.keys().fold(keyed.synced_names, |m: Map<String, Int>, k: String|
      if k.starts_with(source ++ "/") { moved_key(m, k, under_destination(k)) } else { m })
  };
  { ..keyed, synced_names: durable, clock: keyed.clock + 1 }
}

/// Takes the lock at `path` by creating its file, and returns `ok: false` if the file already
/// exists, for example because another caller took the lock.
pub fn mem_lock(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, false) {
    Some(key) -> if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing && can_create(tree, key) {
      let made = stored(tree, key, Plain(b""));
      { fs: { ..made, locks: made.locks.insert(key, tree.clock) }, ok: true }
    } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Releases only a lock this tree took. A lock left behind by another holder is broken by removing
/// its file.
pub fn mem_unlock(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, false) {
    Some(key) -> if tree.locks.contains(key) {
      { fs: { ..dropped(tree, key), locks: tree.locks.remove(key) }, ok: true }
    } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// `at` is the absolute path the tree represents, which the fake has no disk to provide.
pub fn mem_canonical(tree: MemFs, at: String, path: String) -> Option<String> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing { None }
  else if key == "" { Some(at) }
  else { Some(at ++ "/" ++ key) }
}

/// What `fs.mode` returns over the tree.
pub fn mem_mode(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Mode> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing { None } else { Some(mode_at(tree, key)) }
}

/// What `fs.set_mode` does to the tree.
pub fn mem_set_mode(tree: MemFs, path: String, mode: Mode) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, true) {
    Some(key) -> if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing { { fs: tree, ok: false } }
    else { { fs: with_mode(tree, key, mode), ok: true } },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// Creates a symbolic link at `path` that points to `target`. Panics with the host's message if
/// `target`, resolved from the directory containing the link, is the tree's root itself or lies
/// outside the tree.
pub fn mem_symlink(tree: MemFs, path: String, target: String) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, false) {
    Some(key) -> match resolve_target(segments(parent_of(key)), target) {
      Some(_) -> if target != "" && kind_at(tree, key) == Missing && can_create(tree, key) {
        { fs: with_id({ ..tree, links: tree.links.insert(key, target) }, key), ok: true }
      } else { { fs: tree, ok: false } },
      None -> panic(leaves_root_message(target)),
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

/// What `fs.link` does to the tree: `path` becomes another name for the file at `to`, and neither
/// is followed if it is a link.
pub fn mem_link(tree: MemFs, path: String, to: String) -> LinkResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, to, false) {
    None -> { fs: tree, done: Err(NotFound) },
    Some(source) -> match key_of(tree, path, false) {
      None -> { fs: tree, done: Err(NotFound) },
      Some(destination) -> match link_error(tree, source, destination) {
        Some(reason) -> { fs: tree, done: Err(reason) },
        None -> { fs: with_hard_link(with_id(tree, source), source, destination), done: Ok(()) },
      },
    },
  }

fn link_error(tree: MemFs, source: String, destination: String) -> Option<FileError> =
  match kind_at(tree, source) {
    Missing -> Some(NotFound),
    File -> if kind_at(tree, destination) != Missing { Some(Exists) }
    else { create_error(tree, destination) },
    _ -> Some(NotAFile),
  }

fn with_hard_link(tree: MemFs, source: String, destination: String) -> MemFs = {
  let carried = |m: Map<String, Int>|
    match m.get(source) { Some(x) -> m.insert(destination, x), None -> m };
  let id = match tree.ids.get(source) { Some(n) -> n, None -> 0 };
  {
    ..tree,
    files: match tree.files.get(source) {
      Some(b) -> tree.files.insert(destination, b),
      None -> tree.files,
    },
    modes: match tree.modes.get(source) {
      Some(m) -> tree.modes.insert(destination, m),
      None -> tree.modes,
    },
    modified: carried(tree.modified),
    ids: carried(tree.ids),
    shared: tree.shared.insert(id, match tree.shared.get(id) { Some(n) -> n + 1, None -> 2 }),
  }
}

/// What `fs.stat` returns over the tree. A time is the tree's clock, read as milliseconds, or the
/// milliseconds `mem_set_modified` stamped, and every file is on device zero.
pub fn mem_stat(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Stat> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, false)?;
  stat_at(tree, key)
}

fn stat_at(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Option<Stat> = {
  let kind = kind_at(tree, key);
  let id = match tree.ids.get(key) { Some(n) -> n, None -> 0 };
  let stamp = match tree.modified.get(key) {
    Some(at) -> at,
    None -> match tree.dirs.get(key) { Some(at) -> at, None -> 0 },
  };
  if kind == Missing { None } else {
    Some({
      kind: kind,
      size: match tree.files.get(key) { Some(body) -> body_size(body), None -> 0 },
      modified: Instant(stamp *| 1000000),
      mode: if kind == Symlink { full_access_mode() } else { mode_at(tree, key) },
      links: match tree.shared.get(id) { Some(names) -> names, None -> 1 },
      device: 0,
      id: id,
    })
  }
}

/// What `fs.scan` returns over the tree, in `mem_walk`'s order.
pub fn mem_scan(tree: MemFs, path: String, deep: Bool) -> Option<List<ScanEntry>> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) != Dir { None }
  else { Some(scan_tree(tree, key, normalize(path), deep, [], tree.dirs.len())) }
}

fn scan_tree(
  tree: MemFs,
  key: String,
  named: String,
  deep: Bool,
  accumulator: List<ScanEntry>,
  room: Int,
) -> List<ScanEntry> / {abort.raise} =
  if !readable(tree, key) { accumulator } else {
    names_in(tree, key).fold(accumulator, |out: List<ScanEntry>, name: String| {
      let child = under(key, name);
      let shown = under(named, name);
      match stat_at(tree, child) {
        None -> out,
        Some(found) -> {
          let listed = out.push({ path: shown, stat: found });
          if !deep || found.kind != Dir { listed }
          else if room <= 0 { panic("a directory contains itself through a link or a cycle") }
          else { scan_tree(tree, child, shown, deep, listed, room - 1) }
        },
      }
    })
  }

/// Returns the free space the tree reports for the file system containing `path`, or `None` if
/// `path` does not exist. Every path reports the tree's `space` field.
pub fn mem_space(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<Space> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, true)?;
  if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing { None } else { Some(tree.space) }
}

/// What `fs.read_link` returns over the tree.
pub fn mem_read_link(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Option<String> / {abort.raise} = {
  let key = key_of(tree, path, false)?;
  tree.links.get(key)
}

/// A time before the epoch panics, as the host rejects one.
pub fn mem_set_modified(tree: MemFs, path: String, ms: Int) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  if ms < 0 { panic("a modification time is milliseconds since the Unix epoch, never before it") }
  else {
    match key_of(tree, path, true) {
      Some(key) -> if kind_at(tree, key) == Missing { { fs: tree, ok: false } } else {
        {
          fs: {
            ..tree,
            modified: names_of(tree, key).fold(tree.modified, |m: Map<String, Int>, k: String|
              m.insert(k, ms)),
          },
          ok: true,
        }
      },
      None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
    }
  }

/// Opens the file at `path` and returns a descriptor. `ToRead` needs a file its owner may read.
/// `ToWrite` and `ToAppend` need a file its owner may write, and create it if it does not exist;
/// `ToWrite` also truncates it. `ToCreate` follows no link at the path, fails with `Exists` if
/// anything is there, and creates the file `rw-------`. A descriptor refers to a path, not to the
/// file that was there when it was opened. If that file is removed, reads return an empty result
/// (end of file) and writes do nothing. If it is renamed, reads and writes go to whatever now has
/// the old path. A real disk keeps using the original file until it is closed.
pub fn mem_open(tree: MemFs, path: String, how: OpenMode) -> OpenResult / {abort.raise} =
  match key_of(tree, path, how != ToCreate) {
    None -> { fs: tree, file: Err(NotFound) },
    Some(key) -> match prepare_open(tree, key, how) {
      Ok(ready) -> descriptor(ready, key, how),
      Err(reason) -> { fs: tree, file: Err(reason) },
    },
  }

fn prepare_open(tree: MemFs, key: String, how: OpenMode) -> Result<MemFs, FileError> = {
  let kind = kind_at(tree, key);
  if how == ToCreate && kind != Missing { Err(Exists) }
  else if kind == Dir { Err(NotAFile) } else if kind == File {
    if how == ToRead { if readable(tree, key) { Ok(tree) } else { Err(Denied) } }
    else if !writable(tree, key) { Err(Denied) }
    else if how == ToWrite { Ok(stored(tree, key, Plain(b""))) }
    else { Ok(tree) }
  } else if how == ToRead { Err(NotFound) } else {
    match create_error(tree, key) {
      Some(reason) -> Err(reason),
      None ->
        Ok(if how == ToCreate { with_mode(stored(tree, key, Plain(b"")), key, owner_only()) }
        else { stored(tree, key, Plain(b"")) }),
    }
  }
}

fn create_error(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Option<FileError> =
  if key == "" { Some(Exists) } else {
    match ancestors(parent_of(key)).find(|dir: String| kind_at(tree, dir) != Dir) {
      Some(dir) -> Some(if kind_at(tree, dir) == Missing { NotFound } else { NotADirectory }),
      None -> if writable(tree, parent_of(key)) { None } else { Some(Denied) },
    }
  }

fn descriptor(tree: MemFs, key: String, how: OpenMode) -> OpenResult =
  {
    fs: {
      ..tree,
      opened: tree.opened.insert(tree.clock, { key: key, how: how, at: 0 }),
      clock: tree.clock + 1,
    },
    file: Ok(tree.clock),
  }

/// What `fs.read_chunk` returns over the tree: the next bytes, at most `max` and at most the tree's
/// `short` when that is set. So a result shorter than `max` is not the end, and an empty one is. A
/// negative `max` panics, as the host rejects one.
pub fn mem_read_chunk(tree: MemFs, file: Int, max: Int) -> ReadChunkResult / {abort.raise} =
  if max < 0 { panic("a chunk's length cannot be negative") } else {
    match tree.opened.get(file) {
      Some(o) -> if o.how != ToRead || is_sealed(tree, o.key) { { fs: tree, bytes: None } } else {
        let body = match tree.files.get(o.key) { Some(Plain(b)) -> b, _ -> b"" };
        let size = body.len();
        let from = at_most(o.at, size);
        let wanted = if tree.short > 0 { at_most(max, tree.short) } else { max };
        let to = at_most(from + wanted, size);
        {
          fs: { ..tree, opened: tree.opened.insert(file, { ..o, at: to }) },
          bytes: Some(body.slice(from, to)),
        }
      },
      None -> { fs: tree, bytes: None },
    }
  }

/// Writes `body` at the descriptor's position, which is the end of the file for a descriptor opened
/// with `ToAppend`. A file written by `fs.write_secret` accepts bytes only at its end, because the
/// fake can append to it without being able to read it.
pub fn mem_write_chunk(tree: MemFs, file: Int, body: Bytes) -> WriteResult / {abort.raise} =
  match tree.opened.get(file) {
    Some(o) -> if o.how == ToRead { { fs: tree, ok: false } } else {
      match tree.files.get(o.key) {
        Some(Plain(had)) -> {
          let size = had.len();
          let from = if o.how == ToAppend { size } else { at_most(o.at, size) };
          let to = from + body.len();
          let put = stored(
            tree,
            o.key,
            Plain(
              Bytes::concat_all([had.slice(0, from), body, had.slice(at_most(to, size), size)]),
            ),
          );
          { fs: { ..put, opened: put.opened.insert(file, { ..o, at: to }) }, ok: true }
        },
        Some(Sealed(had)) -> if o.how != ToAppend && o.at != body_size(Sealed(had)) {
          { fs: tree, ok: false }
        } else {
          let put = stored(tree, o.key, appended(Sealed(had), body));
          let to = body_size(Sealed(had)) + body.len();
          { fs: { ..put, opened: put.opened.insert(file, { ..o, at: to }) }, ok: true }
        },
        None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
      }
    },
    None -> { fs: tree, ok: false },
  }

fn is_sealed(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Bool =
  match tree.files.get(key) { Some(Sealed(_)) -> true, _ -> false }

/// What `fs.close` does to the tree.
pub fn mem_close(tree: MemFs, file: Int) -> WriteResult =
  if tree.opened.contains(file) { { fs: { ..tree, opened: tree.opened.remove(file) }, ok: true } }
  else { { fs: tree, ok: false } }

/// The tree, returning at most `n` bytes for each `read_chunk` from now on, or as many as requested
/// when `n` is zero. These are the short reads a pipe or a socket gives, which a file on disk
/// rarely does, so a test of a reader runs it over a tree that gives them.
pub fn mem_short_reads(tree: MemFs, n: Int) -> MemFs = { ..tree, short: n }

/// What `go` returned over the fake, and the tree it left.
pub type MemResult<a> = { value: a, tree: MemFs }

/// Runs `go` over the in-memory file system `tree`, handling every file system operation with the
/// fake, and returns `go`'s result and the final tree. Tests pass this the code under test, which
/// may perform any read or write operation on the root `l`. Paths that `canonical` returns for the
/// fake are under `/mem`.
pub fn on_mem<a, [l] | e>(
  tree: MemFs,
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l] | e},
) -> MemResult<a> / {abort.raise | e bounded} = {
  let ran = run_on_mem[l](tree, -1, go);
  { value: ran.value, tree: ran.tree }
}

/// What `go` returned over the fake, the tree it left, and how many operations it performed.
pub type CountedRunResult<a> = { value: a, tree: MemFs, operations: Int }

/// Runs `go` as `on_mem` does, and also counts the operations it performed on the file system, a
/// read counting as much as a write. A test uses this to check the cost of a run.
pub fn on_mem_counted<a, [l] | e>(
  tree: MemFs,
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l] | e},
) -> CountedRunResult<a> / {abort.raise | e bounded} = run_on_mem[l](tree, -1, go)

/// Runs `go` as `on_mem` does, over a disk that fails after `writes` operations that change the
/// tree. Once `writes` such operations have run, every operation that would change the tree changes
/// nothing and returns a failure (`false`, `None`, or `Other` with the input/output error number).
/// A sync, and an `open` that creates or truncates a file, count as changing operations. A read, a
/// `read_chunk` and a `close` do not, and keep working. The returned tree is the state after the
/// first `writes` changes, whatever `go` did to clean up afterwards.
pub fn on_mem_failing<a, [l] | e>(
  tree: MemFs,
  writes: Int,
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l] | e},
) -> MemResult<a> / {abort.raise | e bounded} = {
  let ran = run_on_mem[l](tree, max(writes, 0), go);
  { value: ran.value, tree: ran.tree }
}

fn failed() -> FileError = Other(5, "input/output error")

fn run_on_mem<a, [l] | e>(
  tree: MemFs,
  left: Int,
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l] | e},
) -> CountedRunResult<a> / {abort.raise | e bounded} = {
  let apply_write = |r: MemRun, w: WriteResult| {
    let c = apply_change(r, w.fs);
    { run: c.run, ok: c.made && w.ok }
  };
  handle {
    go()
  } with {
    var run: MemRun = { tree: tree, left: left, operations: 0 },
    fs.read_file[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_read(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.read_at[l](p, offset, len) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_read_at(run.tree, p, offset, len)
    },
    fs.list_dir[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_list(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.kind[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_kind(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.resolved_kind[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_resolved_kind(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.exists[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_exists(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.file_size[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_size(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.modified_ms[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_modified(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.write_file[l](p, body) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_write(run.tree, p, body));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.write_secret[l](p, before, secret, encoding, after) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(
        run,
        mem_write_secret(run.tree, p, {
          before: before,
          secret: secret,
          encoding: encoding,
          after: after,
        }),
      );
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.read_secret[l](p, before, encoding, after) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_read_secret(run.tree, p, before, encoding, after)
    },
    fs.append[l](p, body) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let a = mem_append(run.tree, p, body);
      let c = apply_change(run, a.fs);
      run = c.run;
      if c.made { a.at } else { None }
    },
    fs.create_dir[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_create_dir(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.remove[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_remove(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.rename[l](from, to) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_rename(run.tree, from, to));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.sync[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_sync(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.lock[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_lock(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.unlock[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_unlock(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.copy[l](from, to) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_copy(run.tree, from, to));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.remove_tree[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_remove_tree(run.tree, p));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.temp_dir[l](dir, prefix) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let m = mem_temp_dir(run.tree, dir, prefix);
      let c = apply_change(run, m.fs);
      run = c.run;
      if c.made { m.path } else { None }
    },
    fs.canonical[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_canonical(run.tree, "/mem", p)
    },
    fs.mode[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_mode(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.set_mode[l](p, mode) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_set_mode(run.tree, p, mode));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.symlink[l](p, target) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_symlink(run.tree, p, target));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.read_link[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_read_link(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.walk[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_walk(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.set_modified[l](p, ms) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_set_modified(run.tree, p, ms));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.open[l](p, how) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let o = mem_open(run.tree, p, how);
      if how == ToRead {
        run = { ..run, tree: o.fs };
        o.file
      } else {
        let c = apply_change(run, o.fs);
        run = c.run;
        if c.made { o.file } else { Err(failed()) }
      }
    },
    fs.read_chunk[l](file, max) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let c = mem_read_chunk(run.tree, file, max);
      run = { ..run, tree: c.fs };
      c.bytes
    },
    fs.write_chunk[l](file, body) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let k = apply_write(run, mem_write_chunk(run.tree, file, body));
      run = k.run;
      k.ok
    },
    fs.close[l](file) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let c = mem_close(run.tree, file);
      run = { ..run, tree: c.fs };
      c.ok
    },
    fs.stat[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_stat(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.scan[l](p, deep) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_scan(run.tree, p, deep)
    },
    fs.space[l](p) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      mem_space(run.tree, p)
    },
    fs.link[l](p, to) -> {
      run = count_operation(run);
      let made = mem_link(run.tree, p, to);
      let c = apply_change(run, made.fs);
      run = c.run;
      if c.made { made.done } else { Err(failed()) }
    },
    return value -> { value: value, tree: run.tree, operations: run.operations },
  }
}

type MemRun = { tree: MemFs, left: Int, operations: Int }

fn count_operation(m: MemRun) -> MemRun = { ..m, operations: m.operations + 1 }

fn apply_change(m: MemRun, next: MemFs) -> { run: MemRun, made: Bool } =
  if m.left == 0 { { run: m, made: false } } else {
    { run: { ..m, tree: next, left: if m.left > 0 { m.left - 1 } else { m.left } }, made: true }
  }

/// Whether a write waits for the disk. `Durable` syncs, so once the write returns, a power loss
/// leaves the new file. `Fast` does not: every reader sees the complete file while the machine
/// stays up, but a power loss may leave the old file, the new one, or a file with the new name and
/// none or only some of its bytes.
pub type Durability = | Durable | Fast

/// A file being written next to the path it will replace: that path, the temporary file's path,
/// and the descriptor the temporary file is written through.
pub type Pending = { path: String, temp: String, file: Int }

/// Begins writing the file at `path` completely or not at all, and returns a `Pending` for it. It
/// creates a temporary file in the same directory, named `.tmp-` followed by twenty-four random hex
/// digits and accessible only to its owner, and tries a new name if that name is taken. Bytes
/// written to the `file` field of the `Pending`, the open descriptor of the temporary file, replace
/// `path` when `commit_atomic` renames the temporary file there; `abandon_atomic` removes it
/// instead. `write_atomic` and `std.io`'s `replacing` each call `begin_atomic`, then
/// `commit_atomic` or `abandon_atomic`.
pub fn begin_atomic<[l]>(
  path: String,
) -> Pending / {fs.open[l], files.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  iterate(0, 8, |tries: Int| {
    let temp = under(parent_of(path), ".tmp-" ++ token_hex(12));
    match fs.open[l](temp, ToCreate) {
      Ok(file) -> Stop({ path: path, temp: temp, file: file }),
      Err(reason) -> if reason == Exists && tries < 7 { Continue(tries + 1) }
      else { files.fail(temp, reason) },
    }
  })

/// Finishes a write that `begin_atomic` began. Under `Durable` it first syncs the temporary file.
/// It then closes the file, sets its mode to `mode` (or else to the mode of the existing file at
/// the path, or `rw-r--r--` if there is none), and renames it over the path. The rename replaces a
/// file or link at the path; it does not write into that file or follow that link. Under `Durable`
/// it then syncs the directory. A step the file system fails raises `files.fail` and is not
/// retried. If the failure comes before the rename, the path still has its old contents. After the
/// rename, the path has the new file, although a power loss may still undo the rename.
pub fn commit_atomic<[l]>(
  p: Pending,
  mode: Option<Mode>,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Unit / {fs.sync[l], fs.close[l], fs.stat[l], fs.set_mode[l], fs.rename[l], files.fail} = {
  let dir = parent_of(p.path);
  let wanted = match mode {
    Some(given) -> given,
    None -> match fs.stat[l](p.path) {
      Some(there) -> if there.kind == File { there.mode } else { default_mode(File) },
      None -> default_mode(File),
    },
  };
  if durability == Durable && !fs.sync[l](p.temp) { files.fail(p.temp, operation_failed("sync")) }
  else { () };
  fs.close[l](p.file);
  if !fs.set_mode[l](p.temp, wanted) { files.fail(p.temp, operation_failed("set_mode")) }
  else { () };
  if !fs.rename[l](p.temp, p.path) { files.fail(p.path, operation_failed("rename")) } else { () };
  if durability == Durable && !fs.sync[l](dir) { files.fail(dir, operation_failed("sync")) }
  else { () }
}

/// Abandons a write that `begin_atomic` began: closes the temporary file and removes it. After a
/// `commit_atomic` that renamed the file there is nothing left to remove, so this is safe to call
/// however the surrounding scope ends.
pub fn abandon_atomic<[l]>(p: Pending) -> Unit / {fs.close[l], fs.remove[l]} = {
  fs.close[l](p.file);
  fs.remove[l](p.temp);
  ()
}

/// The error for an operation that reports only that it failed: `Other`, with no error number and
/// the operation's name.
pub fn operation_failed(operation: String) -> FileError = Other(0, operation)

/// Writes `body` as the whole contents of the file at `path`. A reader sees either the old file or
/// `body`, never a mixture. So does a process that starts after the writing process died at any
/// step. The bytes go to a temporary file next to `path` (`begin_atomic`), which is renamed over
/// `path` (`commit_atomic`), or removed if anything fails or the write is cancelled. `mode` is the
/// new file's mode, and `None` uses the mode of the file being replaced.
///
/// If the writing process dies before the rename, `path` is unchanged and a temporary file that
/// nothing reads remains, empty or partly written. If it dies after the rename, `path` is the new
/// file.
///
/// With `Durable`, a power loss before the rename leaves the old file intact. After the rename, a
/// power loss leaves either the old file or the new one. Once the directory is synced, which
/// happens before this function returns, a power loss leaves the new file. With `Fast`, nothing is
/// synced, so a power loss after the rename may leave the new name on a file whose bytes never
/// reached the disk.
pub fn write_atomic<[l]>(
  path: String,
  body: Bytes,
  durability: Durability,
  mode: Option<Mode> = None,
) -> Unit / {fs.open[l], fs.write_chunk[l], fs.space[l], fs.sync[l], fs.close[l], fs.stat[l], fs.set_mode[l], fs.rename[l], fs.remove[l], files.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  bracket(|| begin_atomic[l](path), |p: Pending| abandon_atomic[l](p), |p: Pending|
    if fs.write_chunk[l](p.file, body) { commit_atomic[l](p, mode, durability) }
    else { files.fail(p.temp, write_error[l](p.temp, body.len())) })

fn write_error<[l]>(path: String, n: Int) -> FileError / {fs.space[l]} =
  match fs.space[l](parent_of(path)) {
    Some(room) -> if room.available < n { NoSpace } else { operation_failed("write_chunk") },
    None -> operation_failed("write_chunk"),
  }

/// What a copy does where its destination already contains something.
pub type Existing =
  /// Nothing is copied over anything: the copy fails with `Exists`.
  | Reject
  /// An existing directory is copied into, and an existing file or link is removed and recreated;
  /// anything the source does not name stays. A directory where the source has a file or a link
  /// fails with `NotAFile`: a merge removes no tree.
  | Merge
  /// What is there is removed first, so the destination contains the source's tree and nothing
  /// else.
  | Replace

/// Chooses what a copy does with a link whose target is absolute or climbs out of the destination's
/// root. The target is resolved relative to the directory where the link is placed in the copy, not
/// in the source. No root may contain such a link (`E0452`), so the copy cannot create it as
/// written. The choices are to fail the copy or to leave the link out.
pub type OutsideLink =
  /// The copy fails with `CrossesRoot`.
  | RejectLink
  /// The link is left out, and the copy reports which links it left out.
  | SkipLink

/// How a tree is copied: what to do where the destination contains something, what to do with a
/// link that would point out of the destination's root, and the most entries and file bytes the
/// tree may contain (`LimitExceeded` past either, before anything is copied).
pub type CopyOptions = { existing: Existing, outside_link: OutsideLink, entries: Int, bytes: Int }

/// What a copy passes its fold after each entry: the entry's path under the destination, its kind
/// and a file's size, and the entries and bytes copied so far, including this one.
pub type Progress = { path: String, kind: Kind, size: Int, entries: Int, bytes: Int }

/// The outcome of a copy: what its fold returned, the entries and bytes it copied, the links it
/// left out, and whether its fold stopped it.
pub type CopyResult<a> = {
  value: a,
  entries: Int,
  bytes: Int,
  skipped: List<String>,
  stopped: Bool,
}

type CopyState<a> = {
  done: CopyResult<a>,
  dirs: List<ScanEntry>,
  first_copies: Map<Int, { device: Int, path: String }>,
}

type CopyPlan = {
  source: String,
  destination: String,
  entries: List<ScanEntry>,
  destination_kinds: Map<String, Kind>,
}

type PreparedEntry<a> = {
  copy: CopyState<a>,
  from: String,
  to: String,
  needs_bytes: Bool,
  skipped: Bool,
}

/// Copies the tree at `from` to `to`, both under the root `l`. It copies directories, files with
/// their bytes, modes and modification times (in milliseconds, never before the epoch), and links
/// as links with their targets unchanged. Paths that are hard links to the same file in the source
/// are hard links to one file in the copy.
///
/// Nothing is followed: a link in the source is copied as a link, never read through, so the copy
/// contains nothing that is not in the source tree. Each file is copied by `fs.copy`, which shares
/// blocks where the file system can and copies them otherwise; the result is the same either way.
///
/// `step` is called with each entry after it is copied. It returns `Continue` to go on or `Stop` to
/// end the copy there; what was already copied stays. A directory's mode and time are set after
/// everything beneath it is copied. A file system failure raises `files.fail` with the path and
/// leaves what was copied so far. With `Replace`, the destination's old contents are removed before
/// any copying starts, so a failure leaves a partial copy. The root itself is never replaced.
pub fn copy_tree<a, [l] | e>(
  from: String,
  to: String,
  how: CopyOptions,
  init: a,
  step: (a, Progress) -> Iter<a, a> / e,
) -> CopyResult<a> / {fs.stat[l], fs.scan[l], fs.read_link[l], fs.create_dir[l], fs.remove[l], fs.remove_tree[l], fs.symlink[l], fs.link[l], fs.set_mode[l], fs.set_modified[l], fs.copy[l], files.fail, abort.raise | e} = {
  let found = plan_copy[l, l](from, to, how);
  let copied = found.entries.fold(empty_copy(init), |c: CopyState<a>, entry: ScanEntry|
    if c.done.stopped { c } else {
      let p = placed[l, l](found, how, c, entry);
      if p.skipped { p.copy } else {
        apply_step(
          if p.needs_bytes {
            finish_file_copy[l](p.copy, p.to, entry.stat, fs.copy[l](p.from, p.to))
          } else { p.copy },
          p.to,
          entry.stat,
          step,
        )
      }
    });
  finished[l](copied)
}

/// Copies the tree at `from` under the root `s` to `to` under the root `d`, as `copy_tree` does
/// within a root, except for a file's bytes. Those are read through one descriptor and written
/// through another a mebibyte at a time, so a file of any size is copied using the memory of one
/// chunk, and no blocks are shared.
pub fn copy_tree_across<a, [s], [d] | e>(
  from: String,
  to: String,
  how: CopyOptions,
  init: a,
  step: (a, Progress) -> Iter<a, a> / e,
) -> CopyResult<a> / {fs.stat[s], fs.scan[s], fs.read_link[s], fs.open[s], fs.read_chunk[s], fs.close[s], fs.stat[d], fs.scan[d], fs.create_dir[d], fs.remove[d], fs.remove_tree[d], fs.symlink[d], fs.link[d], fs.set_mode[d], fs.set_modified[d], fs.open[d], fs.write_chunk[d], fs.close[d], files.fail, abort.raise | e} = {
  let found = plan_copy[s, d](from, to, how);
  let copied = found.entries.fold(empty_copy(init), |c: CopyState<a>, entry: ScanEntry|
    if c.done.stopped { c } else {
      let p = placed[s, d](found, how, c, entry);
      if p.skipped { p.copy } else {
        apply_step(
          if p.needs_bytes {
            finish_file_copy[d](
              p.copy,
              p.to,
              entry.stat,
              copy_file_across[s, d](p.from, p.to, entry.stat),
            )
          } else { p.copy },
          p.to,
          entry.stat,
          step,
        )
      }
    });
  finished[d](copied)
}

fn copy_chunk_size() -> Int = 0x10_0000

fn copy_file_across<[s], [d]>(
  from: String,
  to: String,
  found: Stat,
) -> Bool / {fs.open[s], fs.read_chunk[s], fs.close[s], fs.open[d], fs.write_chunk[d], fs.close[d], fs.set_mode[d], abort.raise} =
  bracket(
    || fs.open[s](from, ToRead),
    |opened: Result<Int, FileError>|
      match opened {
        Ok(file) -> {
          fs.close[s](file);
          ()
        },
        Err(_) -> (),
      },
    |opened: Result<Int, FileError>|
      match opened {
        Err(_) -> false,
        Ok(source) ->
          bracket(
              || fs.open[d](to, ToCreate),
              |made: Result<Int, FileError>|
                match made {
                  Ok(file) -> {
                    fs.close[d](file);
                    ()
                  },
                  Err(_) -> (),
                },
              |made: Result<Int, FileError>|
                match made {
                  Err(_) -> false,
                  Ok(sink) ->
                    iterate(0, found.size + 1, |sent: Int|
                      if sent >= found.size { Stop(true) } else {
                        match fs.read_chunk[s](source, min(copy_chunk_size(), found.size - sent)) {
                          None -> Stop(false),
                          Some(bytes) -> if bytes.len() == 0 { Stop(true) }
                          else if fs.write_chunk[d](sink, bytes) { Continue(sent + bytes.len()) }
                          else { Stop(false) },
                        }
                      }),
                },
            )
            && fs.set_mode[d](to, found.mode),
      },
  )

fn below(top: String, rest: String) -> String = if rest == "" { top } else { under(top, rest) }

fn empty_copy<a>(init: a) -> CopyState<a> =
  {
    done: { value: init, entries: 0, bytes: 0, skipped: [], stopped: false },
    dirs: [],
    first_copies: Map::new(),
  }

fn plan_copy<[s], [d]>(
  from: String,
  to: String,
  how: CopyOptions,
) -> CopyPlan / {fs.stat[s], fs.scan[s], fs.stat[d], fs.scan[d], fs.remove_tree[d], files.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let source = normalize(from);
  let destination = normalize(to);
  let beneath = |top: String, seen: List<ScanEntry>|
    seen.map(|entry: ScanEntry|
      { ..entry, path: entry.path.slice(under(top, "").len(), entry.path.len()) });
  let top = match fs.stat[s](source) { Some(found) -> found, None -> files.fail(from, NotFound) };
  let rest = if top.kind != Dir { [] } else {
    match fs.scan[s](source, true) {
      Some(seen) -> beneath(source, seen),
      None -> files.fail(from, NotADirectory),
    }
  };
  let entries = [{ path: "", stat: top }].concat(rest);
  let sized = entries.fold(0, |total: Int, entry: ScanEntry| total +| entry.stat.size);
  if entries.len() > how.entries || sized > how.bytes { files.fail(from, LimitExceeded) }
  else { () };
  let nothing: Map<String, Kind> = Map::new();
  let destination_kinds = match fs.stat[d](destination) {
    None -> nothing,
    Some(found) -> match how.existing {
      Reject -> files.fail(to, Exists),
      Replace -> if fs.remove_tree[d](destination) { nothing }
      else { files.fail(to, operation_failed("remove_tree")) },
      Merge -> if found.kind != Dir { nothing.insert("", found.kind) } else {
        match fs.scan[d](destination, true) {
          Some(seen) ->
            beneath(destination, seen).fold(
              nothing.insert("", Dir),
              |m: Map<String, Kind>, entry: ScanEntry| m.insert(entry.path, entry.stat.kind),
            ),
          None -> files.fail(to, NotADirectory),
        }
      },
    },
  };
  {
    source: source,
    destination: destination,
    entries: entries,
    destination_kinds: destination_kinds,
  }
}

fn placed<a, [s], [d]>(
  found: CopyPlan,
  how: CopyOptions,
  c: CopyState<a>,
  entry: ScanEntry,
) -> PreparedEntry<a> / {fs.read_link[s], fs.remove[d], fs.create_dir[d], fs.symlink[d], fs.link[d], files.fail} = {
  let source = below(found.source, entry.path);
  let destination = below(found.destination, entry.path);
  let before = found.destination_kinds.get(entry.path);
  let done = |copy: CopyState<a>|
    { copy: copy, from: source, to: destination, needs_bytes: false, skipped: false };
  match before {
    None -> (),
    Some(kind) -> if kind != Dir {
      if fs.remove[d](destination) { () }
      else { files.fail(destination, operation_failed("remove")) }
    } else if entry.stat.kind != Dir { files.fail(destination, NotAFile) }
    else { () },
  };
  match entry.stat.kind {
    Dir -> if before == Some(Dir) || fs.create_dir[d](destination) {
      done({ ..c, dirs: c.dirs.push({ path: destination, stat: entry.stat }) })
    } else { files.fail(destination, operation_failed("create_dir")) },
    Symlink -> match fs.read_link[s](source) {
      None -> files.fail(source, NotFound),
      Some(target) -> if resolve_target(segments(parent_of(destination)), target) == None {
        match how.outside_link {
          RejectLink -> files.fail(destination, CrossesRoot),
          SkipLink ->
            {
              ..done({ ..c, done: { ..c.done, skipped: c.done.skipped.push(destination) } }),
              skipped: true,
            },
        }
      } else if fs.symlink[d](destination, target) { done(c) }
      else { files.fail(destination, operation_failed("symlink")) },
    },
    _ -> match c.first_copies.get(entry.stat.id) {
      None -> { ..done(c), needs_bytes: true },
      Some(first) -> if first.device != entry.stat.device { { ..done(c), needs_bytes: true } }
      else {
        match fs.link[d](destination, first.path) {
          Ok(_) -> done(c),
          Err(reason) -> files.fail(destination, reason),
        }
      },
    },
  }
}

fn finish_file_copy<a, [d]>(
  c: CopyState<a>,
  destination: String,
  found: Stat,
  written: Bool,
) -> CopyState<a> / {fs.set_modified[d], files.fail} =
  if !written { files.fail(destination, operation_failed("copy")) }
  else if !fs.set_modified[d](destination, stamp_ms(found)) {
    files.fail(destination, operation_failed("set_modified"))
  } else if found.links < 2 { c } else {
    {
      ..c,
      first_copies: c.first_copies.insert(found.id, { device: found.device, path: destination }),
    }
  }

fn apply_step<a | e>(
  c: CopyState<a>,
  destination: String,
  found: Stat,
  step: (a, Progress) -> Iter<a, a> / e,
) -> CopyState<a> / e = {
  let entries = c.done.entries + 1;
  let bytes = c.done.bytes + found.size;
  let now = {
    path: destination,
    kind: found.kind,
    size: found.size,
    entries: entries,
    bytes: bytes,
  };
  match step(c.done.value, now) {
    Continue(value) -> { ..c, done: { ..c.done, value: value, entries: entries, bytes: bytes } },
    Stop(value) ->
      { ..c, done: { ..c.done, value: value, entries: entries, bytes: bytes, stopped: true } },
  }
}

fn finished<a, [d]>(
  c: CopyState<a>,
) -> CopyResult<a> / {fs.set_modified[d], fs.set_mode[d], files.fail} = {
  c.dirs.reverse().fold((), |_: Unit, dir: ScanEntry|
    if !fs.set_modified[d](dir.path, stamp_ms(dir.stat)) {
      files.fail(dir.path, operation_failed("set_modified"))
    } else if !fs.set_mode[d](dir.path, dir.stat.mode) {
      files.fail(dir.path, operation_failed("set_mode"))
    } else { () });
  c.done
}

fn stamp_ms(found: Stat) -> Int = max(nanos_at(found.modified) / 1000000, 0)

/// One change found by comparing a scan of a directory with the previous scan.
pub type Change =
  /// Something is at the path where nothing was.
  | Created(String)
  /// What is at the path is a different file than before, or the same one with a different size,
  /// time or mode. A directory is modified when its mode changes or when it is a different
  /// directory: what was created or removed in it is reported by its own change.
  | Modified(String)
  /// Nothing is at the path where something was.
  | Removed(String)
  /// What was at the first path is at the second: the file `stat` gives the same `device` and `id`,
  /// with the same size and time. A directory's move is reported once, and not again for each path
  /// that moved with it.
  | Renamed(String, String)
  /// The watch can no longer report precise changes: the directory could not be read, it became
  /// readable after being unreadable, or more changes happened than the watch's limit. Anything
  /// under the directory may have changed, so a program that caches what it read there should read
  /// all of it again.
  | Rescan

/// A watch on a directory: its path, whether everything beneath it is watched or only its own
/// entries, the most changes one look reports before it reports `Rescan` instead, and what the
/// last look saw, `None` if the directory could not be read.
pub type Watch = { path: String, deep: Bool, limit: Int, seen: Option<List<ScanEntry>> }

/// The result of one scan: the updated watch, and the changes since the previous scan.
pub type PollResult = { watch: Watch, changes: List<Change> }

/// Returns a watch on the directory at `path`, starting from the directory's current state. A watch
/// is a plain value: nothing is registered with the host or released, and each poll is one
/// `fs.scan`, so every poll reads every watched entry. A change is reported by the next poll after
/// it. Two changes to one path between polls are reported as one, and a change that is undone
/// between polls is not reported. A file rewritten to the same size within one tick of the file
/// system's clock is not detected. Watching a file system mounted from another machine works the
/// same way.
pub fn watch<[l]>(path: String, deep: Bool, limit: Int) -> Watch / {fs.scan[l]} =
  { path: path, deep: deep, limit: limit, seen: fs.scan[l](path, deep) }

/// Looks once, without waiting: what changed since the watch's last look.
pub fn poll<[l]>(w: Watch) -> PollResult / {fs.scan[l], abort.raise} =
  apply_scan(w, fs.scan[l](w.path, w.deep))

/// Returns the watch updated with the scan result `now`, and the changes since the previous scan.
/// `now` is `None` when the directory could not be read. A directory that becomes unreadable, or
/// readable again after being unreadable, gives `Rescan`, never an empty directory. If the changes
/// exceed the watch's limit, the result is `Rescan`.
pub fn apply_scan(w: Watch, now: Option<List<ScanEntry>>) -> PollResult / {abort.raise} = {
  let next = { ..w, seen: now };
  match now {
    None -> { watch: next, changes: if w.seen == None { [] } else { [Rescan] } },
    Some(after) -> match w.seen {
      None -> { watch: next, changes: [Rescan] },
      Some(before) -> {
        let found = changes(before, after);
        { watch: next, changes: if found.len() > w.limit { [Rescan] } else { found } }
      },
    },
  }
}

/// Waits for the directory to change and then to stay unchanged. It sleeps `every` (at least a
/// millisecond) before each look, and returns once a look differs from the watch's last and the
/// next look agrees with it, so a burst of writes gives one result. It looks at most `looks` times;
/// when none are left it returns what it has, which is no change if nothing changed.
pub fn await_changes<[l]>(
  w: Watch,
  every: Duration,
  looks: Int,
) -> PollResult / {fs.scan[l], time.sleep_ms, abort.raise} =
  iterate(
    { left: looks, settling: false, last: w.seen },
    max(looks, 0) + 1,
    |s: { left: Int, settling: Bool, last: Option<List<ScanEntry>> }|
      if s.left <= 0 {
        Stop(if s.settling { apply_scan(w, s.last) } else { { watch: w, changes: [] } })
      } else {
        time.sleep_ms(max(as_millis(every), 1));
        let now = fs.scan[l](w.path, w.deep);
        if now != s.last { Continue({ left: s.left - 1, settling: true, last: now }) }
        else if s.settling && now != w.seen { Stop(apply_scan(w, now)) }
        else { Continue({ left: s.left - 1, settling: false, last: now }) }
      },
  )

type Move = { from: String, to: String, dir: Bool }

/// What changed between two scans of one directory: what was removed, what was renamed, what was
/// created and what was modified, each in scan order.
pub fn changes(before: List<ScanEntry>, after: List<ScanEntry>) -> List<Change> / {abort.raise} = {
  let by_path = |seen: List<ScanEntry>|
    seen.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, Stat>, s: ScanEntry| m.insert(s.path, s.stat));
  let was = by_path(before);
  let is = by_path(after);
  let gone = before.filter(|s: ScanEntry| !is.contains(s.path));
  let came = after.filter(|s: ScanEntry| !was.contains(s.path));
  let moves = moved_between(gone, came);
  let dirs = moves.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, String>, v: Move|
    if v.dir { m.insert(v.from, v.to) } else { m });
  let left = moves.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, String>, v: Move| m.insert(v.from, v.to));
  let arrived = moves.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<String, String>, v: Move| m.insert(v.to, v.from));
  gone.filter(|s: ScanEntry| !left.contains(s.path)).map(|s: ScanEntry| Removed(s.path)).concat(
    moves.filter(|v: Move| !moved_with_parent(dirs, v)).map(|v: Move| Renamed(v.from, v.to)),
  ).concat(
    came.filter(|s: ScanEntry| !arrived.contains(s.path)).map(|s: ScanEntry|
      Created(s.path)).concat(
      after.filter(|s: ScanEntry|
        match was.get(s.path) { Some(old) -> altered(old, s.stat), None -> false }).map(
        |s: ScanEntry| Modified(s.path),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

fn moved_between(gone: List<ScanEntry>, came: List<ScanEntry>) -> List<Move> =
  gone.fold(
    {
      free: came.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<Int, ScanEntry>, s: ScanEntry| m.insert(s.stat.id, s)),
      moves: [],
    },
    |accumulator: { free: Map<Int, ScanEntry>, moves: List<Move> }, g: ScanEntry|
      match accumulator.free.get(g.stat.id) {
        Some(c) -> if !same_file(g.stat, c.stat) { accumulator } else {
          {
            free: accumulator.free.remove(g.stat.id),
            moves: accumulator.moves.push({ from: g.path, to: c.path, dir: g.stat.kind == Dir }),
          }
        },
        None -> accumulator,
      },
  ).moves

fn same_file(a: Stat, b: Stat) -> Bool =
  a.device == b.device && a.id == b.id && a.kind == b.kind
    && (a.kind != File || (a.size == b.size && a.modified == b.modified))

fn moved_with_parent(dirs: Map<String, String>, v: Move) -> Bool / {abort.raise} = {
  let above = ancestors(v.from);
  above.take(above.len() - 1).filter(|dir: String|
      match dirs.get(dir) {
        Some(to) -> v.to == to ++ v.from.slice(dir.len(), v.from.len()),
        None -> false,
      }).len(
    )
    > 0
}

fn altered(old: Stat, new: Stat) -> Bool =
  old.kind != new.kind || old.id != new.id || old.device != new.device || old.mode != new.mode
    || (new.kind == File && (old.size != new.size || old.modified != new.modified))

fn max_link_hops() -> Int = 40

fn key_of(tree: MemFs, path: String, last: Bool) -> Option<String> / {abort.raise} =
  if mem_confined(path) { follow_links(tree, path, segments(path), last, 0) }
  else { panic(leaves_root_message(path)) }

fn leaves_root_message(what: String) -> String = "`" ++ what ++ "` leaves the root it was given"

fn follow_links(
  tree: MemFs,
  path: String,
  segs: List<String>,
  last: Bool,
  hops: Int,
) -> Option<String> / {abort.raise} = {
  let n = segs.len();
  let hit = range(0, n).fold(-1, |found: Int, i: Int|
    if found >= 0 { found }
    else if (i < n - 1 || last) && tree.links.contains(joined(segs.take(i + 1))) { i }
    else { -1 });
  if hit < 0 { Some(joined(segs)) }
  else if hops >= max_link_hops() { None } else {
    let target = tree.links.get(joined(segs.take(hit + 1)))?;
    match resolve_target(segs.take(hit), target) {
      Some(base) -> follow_links(tree, path, base.concat(segs.drop(hit + 1)), last, hops + 1),
      None -> panic(leaves_root_message(path)),
    }
  }
}

fn resolve_target(at: List<String>, target: String) -> Option<List<String>> =
  if target.starts_with("/") { None } else {
    target.split("/").fold(Some(at), |accumulator: Option<List<String>>, s: String|
      match accumulator {
        Some(here) -> if s == "" || s == "." { accumulator }
        else if s == ".." { if here.len() == 0 { None } else { Some(here.take(here.len() - 1)) } }
        else { Some(here.push(s)) },
        None -> None,
      })
  }

fn kind_at(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Kind =
  if key == "" || tree.dirs.contains(key) { Dir }
  else if tree.files.contains(key) { File }
  else if tree.links.contains(key) { Symlink }
  else { Missing }

fn access(read: Bool, write: Bool, execute: Bool) -> Access =
  { read: read, write: write, execute: execute }

fn default_mode(k: Kind) -> Mode =
  match k {
    Dir ->
      {
        owner: access(true, true, true),
        group: access(true, false, true),
        other: access(true, false, true),
      },
    _ ->
      {
        owner: access(true, true, false),
        group: access(true, false, false),
        other: access(true, false, false),
      },
  }

fn mode_at(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Mode =
  match tree.modes.get(key) { Some(m) -> m, None -> default_mode(kind_at(tree, key)) }

fn readable(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Bool = mode_at(tree, key).owner.read

fn writable(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Bool = mode_at(tree, key).owner.write

fn can_create(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Bool =
  key != "" && kind_at(tree, parent_of(key)) == Dir && writable(tree, parent_of(key))

fn can_write_file(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> Bool =
  match kind_at(tree, key) {
    File -> writable(tree, key),
    Missing -> can_create(tree, key),
    _ -> false,
  }

fn owner_only() -> Mode =
  {
    owner: access(true, true, false),
    group: access(false, false, false),
    other: access(false, false, false),
  }

fn full_access_mode() -> Mode =
  {
    owner: access(true, true, true),
    group: access(true, true, true),
    other: access(true, true, true),
  }

fn with_id(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> MemFs =
  if tree.ids.contains(key) { tree }
  else { { ..tree, ids: tree.ids.insert(key, tree.next_id), next_id: tree.next_id + 1 } }

fn names_of(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> List<String> =
  match tree.ids.get(key) {
    Some(id) -> if !tree.shared.contains(id) { [key] }
    else { tree.files.keys().filter(|k: String| tree.ids.get(k) == Some(id)) },
    None -> [key],
  }

fn stored(tree: MemFs, key: String, body: Body) -> MemFs = {
  let named = with_id(tree, key);
  let names = names_of(named, key);
  {
    ..named,
    files: names.fold(named.files, |m: Map<String, Body>, k: String| m.insert(k, body)),
    modified: names.fold(named.modified, |m: Map<String, Int>, k: String| m.insert(k, named.clock)),
    clock: named.clock + 1,
  }
}

fn with_mode(tree: MemFs, key: String, mode: Mode) -> MemFs =
  {
    ..tree,
    modes: names_of(tree, key).fold(tree.modes, |m: Map<String, Mode>, k: String|
      m.insert(k, mode)),
  }

fn dropped(tree: MemFs, key: String) -> MemFs = {
  let names = match tree.ids.get(key) {
    Some(id) -> match tree.shared.get(id) {
      Some(n) -> if n > 2 { tree.shared.insert(id, n - 1) } else { tree.shared.remove(id) },
      None -> tree.shared,
    },
    None -> tree.shared,
  };
  {
    ..tree,
    files: tree.files.remove(key),
    dirs: tree.dirs.remove(key),
    links: tree.links.remove(key),
    modes: tree.modes.remove(key),
    modified: tree.modified.remove(key),
    ids: tree.ids.remove(key),
    shared: names,
  }
}

fn rekeyed(tree: MemFs, from: String, to: String) -> MemFs =
  {
    ..tree,
    files: moved_key(tree.files, from, to),
    dirs: moved_key(tree.dirs, from, to),
    links: moved_key(tree.links, from, to),
    modes: moved_key(tree.modes, from, to),
    modified: moved_key(tree.modified, from, to),
    locks: moved_key(tree.locks, from, to),
    ids: moved_key(tree.ids, from, to),
  }

fn moved_key<v>(m: Map<String, v>, from: String, to: String) -> Map<String, v> =
  match m.get(from) { Some(x) -> m.remove(from).insert(to, x), None -> m }

fn normalize(p: String) -> String = joined(segments(p))

fn joined(segs: List<String>) -> String =
  segs.fold("", |accumulator: String, s: String|
    if accumulator == "" { s } else { accumulator ++ "/" ++ s })

/// Returns the path of `name` inside `dir`. When `dir` is empty, returns `name` unchanged.
pub fn under(dir: String, name: String) -> String =
  if dir == "" { name } else { dir ++ "/" ++ name }

fn ancestors(p: String) -> List<String> =
  segments(p).fold(
    { at: "", out: [] },
    |accumulator: { at: String, out: List<String> }, s: String| {
      let next = if accumulator.at == "" { s } else { accumulator.at ++ "/" ++ s };
      { at: next, out: accumulator.out.push(next) }
    },
  ).out

fn parent_of(p: String) -> String = {
  let segs = segments(p);
  joined(segs.take(segs.len() - 1))
}

fn base_of(p: String) -> String =
  match segments(p).at(segments(p).len() - 1) { Some(name) -> name, None -> "" }

fn all_keys(tree: MemFs) -> List<String> =
  tree.files.keys().concat(tree.dirs.keys()).concat(tree.links.keys())

fn names_in(tree: MemFs, dir: String) -> List<String> =
  all_keys(tree).fold(Map::new(), |accumulator: Map<String, Bool>, k: String|
    if k != "" && parent_of(k) == dir { accumulator.insert(base_of(k), true) }
    else { accumulator }).keys(
  )

test "`fs` reads run under a handler that serves in-memory files return the same results every time, never touch the disk, and return the fake files' contents" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "lexer.ply", body: b"fn f() -> Int = 1" }]);
  handle {
    assert_eq(fs.read_file[src]("lexer.ply"), Some(b"fn f() -> Int = 1"));
    assert_eq(fs.read_file[src]("absent.ply"), None);
    assert(fs.exists[src]("lexer.ply"));
    assert(!fs.exists[src]("absent.ply"));
    assert_eq(fs.file_size[src]("lexer.ply"), Some(17))
  } with {
    fs.read_file[src](p) -> mem_read(fixture, p),
    fs.exists[src](p) -> mem_exists(fixture, p),
    fs.file_size[src](p) -> mem_size(fixture, p),
  }
}

test "`fs.kind` returns Dir for the root and directories, File for files, and Missing for paths that do not exist" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  handle {
    assert_eq(fs.kind[src](""), Dir);
    assert_eq(fs.kind[src]("src"), Dir);
    assert_eq(fs.kind[src]("src/a.ply"), File);
    assert_eq(fs.kind[src]("src/absent.ply"), Missing)
  } with {
    fs.kind[src](p) -> mem_kind(fixture, p),
  }
}

test "`resolved` returns what a path refers to after a symlink, where `kind` reports the link" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  handle {
    assert_eq(fs.resolved_kind[src]("src"), Dir);
    assert_eq(fs.resolved_kind[src]("src/a.ply"), File);
    assert_eq(fs.resolved_kind[src]("src/absent.ply"), Missing);
    assert_eq(fs.resolved_kind[src]("src"), fs.kind[src]("src"))
  } with {
    fs.kind[src](p) -> mem_kind(fixture, p),
    fs.resolved_kind[src](p) -> mem_resolved_kind(fixture, p),
  }
}

test "a handled fs write is deterministic and the next read sees it" {
  handle {
    assert(fs.create_dir[out]("build/cache"));
    assert(fs.write_file[out]("build/cache/a.plyx", b"artifact"));
    assert_eq(fs.read_file[out]("build/cache/a.plyx"), Some(b"artifact"));
    assert_eq(fs.list_dir[out]("build/cache"), Some([{ name: "a.plyx", kind: File }]));
    assert(fs.rename[out]("build/cache/a.plyx", "build/cache/b.plyx"));
    assert_eq(fs.read_file[out]("build/cache/a.plyx"), None);
    assert_eq(fs.read_file[out]("build/cache/b.plyx"), Some(b"artifact"))
  } with {
    var t = mem_empty(),
    fs.create_dir[out](p) -> {
      let w = mem_create_dir(t, p);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.write_file[out](p, body) -> {
      let w = mem_write(t, p, body);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.rename[out](from, to) -> {
      let w = mem_rename(t, from, to);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.read_file[out](p) -> mem_read(t, p),
    fs.list_dir[out](p) -> mem_list(t, p),
  }
}

fn raised<a>(run: () -> a / {abort.raise}) -> Option<String> =
  handle { run() } with { abort.raise(m) -> Some(m), return v -> None }

fn escapes(path: String) -> Option<String> = Some("`" ++ path ++ "` leaves the root it was given")

test "in-memory filesystem operations raise an escape error for a path that leaves the root" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  assert(mem_confined("src/a.ply"));
  assert(mem_confined("./src/a.ply"));
  assert(!mem_confined("/etc/passwd"));
  assert(!mem_confined("../secrets"));
  assert(!mem_confined("src/../../secrets"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_read(fixture, "../src/a.ply")), escapes("../src/a.ply"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_read(fixture, "/src/a.ply")), escapes("/src/a.ply"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_exists(fixture, "src/../../a.ply")), escapes("src/../../a.ply"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_kind(fixture, "../elsewhere")), escapes("../elsewhere"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_write(fixture, "../out.txt", b"x")), escapes("../out.txt"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_read(fixture, "src/a.ply")), None)
}

test "`mem_list` lists one directory's entries without descending into subdirectories, and returns `None` for a file or missing path" {
  let fixture = mem_of([
    { path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" },
    { path: "src/deep/b.ply", body: b"b" },
    { path: "top.ply", body: b"t" },
  ]);
  assert_eq(
    mem_list(fixture, ""),
    Some([{ name: "src", kind: Dir }, { name: "top.ply", kind: File }]),
  );
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(fixture, "src")), Some(["a.ply", "deep"]));
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(fixture, "src/deep")), Some(["b.ply"]));
  assert_eq(mem_list(fixture, "src/a.ply"), None);
  assert_eq(mem_list(fixture, "nowhere"), None)
}

test "`mem_list` gives each entry the same kind that `mem_kind` returns for its path, including symlinks" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }, { path: "src/deep/b.ply", body: b"b" }]);
  let linked = mem_symlink(fixture, "src/again", "a.ply").fs;
  match mem_list(linked, "src") {
    Some(listed) -> {
      assert_eq(listed_names(Some(listed)), Some(["a.ply", "again", "deep"]));
      assert(listed.all(|l: Listed| l.kind == mem_kind(linked, "src/" ++ l.name)))
    },
    None -> panic("src is a directory"),
  }
}

test "writing a file sets its modification time, reading does not change it, and a later write sets a later time" {
  let one = mem_write(mem_empty(), "a.txt", b"1").fs;
  let two = mem_write(one, "b.txt", b"2").fs;
  match mem_modified(two, "a.txt") {
    None -> panic("the file was written"),
    Some(first) -> match mem_modified(two, "b.txt") {
      None -> panic("the file was written"),
      Some(second) -> {
        assert(second > first);
        assert_eq(mem_read(two, "a.txt"), Some(b"1"));
        assert_eq(mem_modified(two, "a.txt"), Some(first))
      },
    },
  }
}

test "a write needs its directory, and create_dir makes every missing one" {
  assert(!mem_write(mem_empty(), "build/cache/a.plyx", b"x").ok);
  let made = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "build/cache");
  assert(made.ok);
  assert(mem_exists(made.fs, "build"));
  assert(mem_exists(made.fs, "build/cache"));
  assert(mem_write(made.fs, "build/cache/a.plyx", b"x").ok);
  assert(mem_create_dir(made.fs, "build/cache").ok)
}

test "remove takes one file or one empty directory and never a tree" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  assert(!mem_remove(fixture, "src").ok);
  let gone = mem_remove(fixture, "src/a.ply");
  assert(gone.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(gone.fs, "src/a.ply"), None);
  assert_eq(mem_modified(gone.fs, "src/a.ply"), None);
  assert(mem_remove(gone.fs, "src").ok);
  assert(!mem_exists(mem_remove(gone.fs, "src").fs, "src"));
  assert(!mem_remove(fixture, "absent").ok)
}

test "`mem_rename` moves a file, and fails when the source or the destination directory is missing or the destination leaves the root" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "out/a.tmp", body: b"artifact" }]);
  let moved = mem_rename(fixture, "out/a.tmp", "out/a.plyx");
  assert(moved.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(moved.fs, "out/a.tmp"), None);
  assert_eq(mem_read(moved.fs, "out/a.plyx"), Some(b"artifact"));
  let rejected = mem_rename(fixture, "out/a.tmp", "elsewhere/a.plyx");
  assert(!rejected.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(rejected.fs, "out/a.tmp"), Some(b"artifact"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_rename(fixture, "out/a.tmp", "../a.plyx")), escapes("../a.plyx"));
  assert(!mem_rename(fixture, "absent", "out/b.plyx").ok)
}

test "an append creates the file it is given and returns where the bytes were written" {
  let dir = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "cache");
  let first = mem_append(dir.fs, "cache/frontend.dat", b"header");
  assert_eq(first.at, Some(0));
  assert(mem_exists(first.fs, "cache/frontend.dat"));
  let second = mem_append(first.fs, "cache/frontend.dat", b"frame");
  assert_eq(second.at, Some(6));
  assert_eq(mem_read(second.fs, "cache/frontend.dat"), Some(b"headerframe"));
  assert_eq(mem_size(second.fs, "cache/frontend.dat"), Some(11));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(second.fs, "cache/frontend.dat", 6, 5), Some(b"frame"));
  assert_eq(mem_append(mem_empty(), "cache/frontend.dat", b"x").at, None);
  assert_eq(mem_append(dir.fs, "cache", b"x").at, None);
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_append(dir.fs, "../escape.dat", b"x")), escapes("../escape.dat"))
}

test "`write_secret` on the in-memory filesystem stores a secret that `read_file` cannot return and only `read_secret` can read" {
  let key = Secret::of_bytes(b"\x01\x23\x45\x67");
  let tree = mem_of([
    { path: "keys/old", body: b"plain" },
    { path: "keys/typed", body: b"key 01234567\n" },
  ]);
  let ran = on_mem[k](tree, || {
    let fresh = fs.write_secret[k]("keys/new", b"key ", key, "hex", b"\n");
    let over = fs.write_secret[k]("keys/old", b"", key, "raw", b"");
    let nowhere = fs.write_secret[k]("missing/key", b"", key, "raw", b"");
    {
      fresh: fresh,
      over: over,
      nowhere: nowhere,
      read: fs.read_file[k]("keys/new"),
      back: fs.read_secret[k]("keys/new", b"key ", "hex", b"\n"),
      elsewise: fs.read_secret[k]("keys/new", b"key ", "hex", b""),
      typed: fs.read_secret[k]("keys/typed", b"key ", "hex", b"\n"),
      none: fs.read_secret[k]("keys/none", b"", "raw", b""),
    }
  });
  assert(ran.value.fresh && ran.value.over && !ran.value.nowhere);
  assert_eq(ran.value.read, None);
  assert(ran.value.back == Some(key) && ran.value.typed == Some(key));
  assert(ran.value.elsewise == None && ran.value.none == None);
  assert(mem_read_secret(tree, "keys/old", b"", "raw", b"") == Some(Secret::of_bytes(b"plain")));
  assert(mem_read_secret(tree, "keys/old", b"pl", "raw", b"n") == Some(Secret::of_bytes(b"ai")));
  assert(mem_read_secret(tree, "keys/old", b"", "hex", b"") == None);
  assert(mem_read_secret(tree, "keys/typed", b"key ", "hex", b"") == None);
  let is_private_file = |path: String, size: Int|
    mem_size(ran.tree, path) == Some(size) && mem_mode(ran.tree, path) == Some(owner_only());
  assert(is_private_file("keys/new", 13) && is_private_file("keys/old", 4));
  assert(
    mem_framed_secret(ran.tree, "keys/new")
      == Some({ before: b"key ", secret: key, encoding: "hex", after: b"\n" }),
  );
  assert(mem_framed_secret(tree, "keys/old") == None);
  let grown = mem_append(ran.tree, "keys/new", b"!").fs;
  assert(mem_read_secret(grown, "keys/new", b"key ", "hex", b"\n!") == Some(key));
  assert_eq(mem_size(grown, "keys/new"), Some(14));
  let plain = mem_write(grown, "keys/new", b"open").fs;
  assert(mem_framed_secret(plain, "keys/new") == None);
  assert_eq(mem_read(plain, "keys/new"), Some(b"open"))
}

test "`mem_append` to two files in one directory returns each file's own start position, and each file contains only its own bytes" {
  let dir = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "cache");
  let a1 = mem_append(dir.fs, "cache/a.dat", b"aaaa");
  let b1 = mem_append(a1.fs, "cache/b.dat", b"b");
  let a2 = mem_append(b1.fs, "cache/a.dat", b"AA");
  let b2 = mem_append(a2.fs, "cache/b.dat", b"BB");
  assert_eq(a1.at, Some(0));
  assert_eq(b1.at, Some(0));
  assert_eq(a2.at, Some(4));
  assert_eq(b2.at, Some(1));
  assert_eq(mem_read(b2.fs, "cache/a.dat"), Some(b"aaaaAA"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(b2.fs, "cache/b.dat"), Some(b"bBB"))
}

test "a ranged read returns what is there, and a range past the end is short" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "cache/a.dat", body: b"0123456789" }]);
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 0, 10), Some(b"0123456789"));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 3, 4), Some(b"3456"));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 8, 100), Some(b"89"));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 10, 5), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 99, 5), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/a.dat", 4, 0), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache/absent.dat", 0, 4), None);
  assert_eq(mem_read_at(fixture, "cache", 0, 4), None);
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_read_at(fixture, "../secrets", 0, 4)), escapes("../secrets"))
}

test "append, sync and write-then-rename work through a handler backed by the in-memory filesystem" {
  handle {
    assert_eq(fs.append[cache]("cache/frontend.dat", b"frame"), Some(0));
    assert(fs.sync[cache]("cache/frontend.dat"));
    assert(fs.write_file[cache]("cache/frontend.tmp", b"index"));
    assert(fs.rename[cache]("cache/frontend.tmp", "cache/frontend.idx"));
    assert(fs.sync[cache]("cache"));
    assert_eq(fs.read_at[cache]("cache/frontend.dat", 0, 5), Some(b"frame"));
    assert(!fs.sync[cache]("cache/absent.dat"))
  } with {
    var t = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "cache").fs,
    fs.append[cache](p, body) -> {
      let a = mem_append(t, p, body);
      t = a.fs;
      a.at
    },
    fs.write_file[cache](p, body) -> {
      let w = mem_write(t, p, body);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.rename[cache](from, to) -> {
      let w = mem_rename(t, from, to);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.sync[cache](p) -> {
      let w = mem_sync(t, p);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.read_at[cache](p, offset, len) -> mem_read_at(t, p, offset, len),
  }
}

test "a lock has one holder at a time, and only its holder releases it" {
  let dir = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "cache");
  let locked = mem_lock(dir.fs, "cache/lock");
  assert(locked.ok);
  assert(mem_exists(locked.fs, "cache/lock"));
  assert(!mem_lock(locked.fs, "cache/lock").ok);
  assert(!mem_unlock(mem_write(dir.fs, "cache/lock", b"").fs, "cache/lock").ok);
  let freed = mem_unlock(locked.fs, "cache/lock");
  assert(freed.ok);
  assert(!mem_exists(freed.fs, "cache/lock"));
  assert(!mem_unlock(freed.fs, "cache/lock").ok);
  assert(mem_lock(freed.fs, "cache/lock").ok);
  assert(!mem_lock(mem_empty(), "cache/lock").ok);
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_lock(dir.fs, "../lock")), escapes("../lock"))
}

test "through a handler, a second `lock` fails until `unlock` releases the first" {
  handle {
    assert(fs.lock[cache]("cache/lock"));
    assert(!fs.lock[cache]("cache/lock"));
    assert(fs.unlock[cache]("cache/lock"));
    assert(!fs.unlock[cache]("cache/lock"));
    assert(fs.lock[cache]("cache/lock"))
  } with {
    var t = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "cache").fs,
    fs.lock[cache](p) -> {
      let w = mem_lock(t, p);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.unlock[cache](p) -> {
      let w = mem_unlock(t, p);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
  }
}

test "a path with a leading `./`, a doubled `/` or a trailing `/` names the same file as the plain path" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  assert_eq(mem_read(fixture, "./src/a.ply"), Some(b"a"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(fixture, "src//a.ply"), Some(b"a"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(fixture, "src/a.ply/"), Some(b"a"))
}

fn mode_of(text: String) -> Mode / {abort.raise} =
  match mode_of_text(text) { Some(m) -> m, None -> panic(text) }

test "a copy carries a file's bytes and mode, and never a directory" {
  let base = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"x" }]), "d").fs;
  let with_mode = mem_set_mode(base, "a.txt", mode_of("rw-------")).fs;
  let copied = mem_copy(with_mode, "a.txt", "b.txt");
  assert(copied.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(copied.fs, "b.txt"), Some(b"x"));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(copied.fs, "b.txt"), Some(mode_of("rw-------")));
  assert(!mem_copy(with_mode, "d", "e").ok);
  assert(!mem_copy(with_mode, "a.txt", "d").ok);
  assert(!mem_copy(with_mode, "a.txt", "absent/b.txt").ok);
  assert(!mem_copy(with_mode, "absent.txt", "c.txt").ok)
}

test "`mem_remove_tree` deletes a directory and its contents, deletes a symlink inside without following it, and fails for the root" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "t/sub/f.txt", body: b"f" }, { path: "keep.txt", body: b"k" }]);
  let linked = mem_symlink(fixture, "t/link", "../keep.txt").fs;
  let gone = mem_remove_tree(linked, "t");
  assert(gone.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_kind(gone.fs, "t"), Missing);
  assert_eq(mem_kind(gone.fs, "t/sub/f.txt"), Missing);
  assert_eq(mem_read(gone.fs, "keep.txt"), Some(b"k"));
  assert(!mem_remove_tree(linked, "").ok);
  assert(!mem_remove_tree(linked, "absent").ok);
  assert(mem_remove_tree(linked, "keep.txt").ok)
}

test "a temporary directory is new each time and named relative to the root" {
  let base = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "scratch").fs;
  let one = mem_temp_dir(base, "scratch", "t-");
  let two = mem_temp_dir(one.fs, "./scratch/", "t-");
  let first = match one.path { Some(p) -> p, None -> panic("a directory is made") };
  let second = match two.path { Some(p) -> p, None -> panic("and another") };
  assert(first != second);
  assert(first.starts_with("scratch/t-"));
  assert(second.starts_with("scratch/t-"));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(two.fs, first), Dir);
  assert_eq(mem_kind(two.fs, second), Dir);
  assert_eq(mem_temp_dir(base, "scratch", "a/b").path, None);
  assert_eq(mem_temp_dir(base, "absent", "t-").path, None)
}

test "`mem_canonical` returns a path's absolute form with symlinks followed, prefixed by the given base path, and `None` for a missing path" {
  let fixture = mem_symlink(mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"x" }]), "l", "a.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_canonical(fixture, "/w", "a.txt"), Some("/w/a.txt"));
  assert_eq(mem_canonical(fixture, "/w", "./l"), Some("/w/a.txt"));
  assert_eq(mem_canonical(fixture, "/w", ""), Some("/w"));
  assert_eq(mem_canonical(fixture, "/w", "absent"), None)
}

test "a mode is nine bits, and the fake enforces them for its owner" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"x" }]);
  assert_eq(mem_mode(fixture, "a.txt"), Some(mode_of("rw-r--r--")));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(fixture, ""), Some(mode_of("rwxr-xr-x")));
  let locked = mem_set_mode(fixture, "a.txt", mode_of("---------"));
  assert(locked.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(locked.fs, "a.txt"), None);
  assert_eq(mem_size(locked.fs, "a.txt"), Some(1));
  assert(!mem_write(locked.fs, "a.txt", b"y").ok);
  let closed = mem_set_mode(fixture, "", mode_of("r-xr-xr-x")).fs;
  assert(!mem_write(closed, "b.txt", b"y").ok);
  assert(mem_write(closed, "a.txt", b"y").ok);
  assert(!mem_set_mode(fixture, "absent", mode_of("rwxrwxrwx")).ok)
}

test "a mode's text is its nine letters, and nothing else is a mode" {
  assert_eq(mode_text(mode_of("rwxr-x--x")), "rwxr-x--x");
  assert_eq(mode_of_text("rwx"), None);
  assert_eq(mode_of_text("rwxrwxrwz"), None);
  assert_eq(mode_of_text("xwrxwrxwr"), None)
}

test "reads and writes follow a symlink to its target, while `kind`, `read_link` and `remove` act on the symlink itself" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.ply", body: b"a" }]);
  let linked = mem_symlink(mem_symlink(fixture, "here", "src").fs, "src/again", "a.ply").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_kind(linked, "here"), Symlink);
  assert_eq(mem_resolved_kind(linked, "here"), Dir);
  assert_eq(mem_read(linked, "here/a.ply"), Some(b"a"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(linked, "src/again"), Some(b"a"));
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(linked, "here")), Some(["a.ply", "again"]));
  assert_eq(mem_read_link(linked, "src/again"), Some("a.ply"));
  assert_eq(mem_read_link(linked, "src/a.ply"), None);
  let through = mem_write(linked, "src/again", b"b");
  assert(through.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(through.fs, "src/a.ply"), Some(b"b"));
  let unlinked = mem_remove(linked, "here");
  assert(unlinked.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_kind(unlinked.fs, "here"), Missing);
  assert_eq(mem_read(unlinked.fs, "src/a.ply"), Some(b"a"))
}

test "creating a symlink that points outside the root fails, and reading a symlink loop returns `None`" {
  let fixture = mem_create_dir(mem_empty(), "d").fs;
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_symlink(fixture, "out", "../elsewhere")), escapes("../elsewhere"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_symlink(fixture, "d/out", "../../x")), escapes("../../x"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_symlink(fixture, "abs", "/etc")), escapes("/etc"));
  assert(mem_symlink(fixture, "d/up", "../d").ok);
  let back = mem_symlink(fixture, "d/back", "..").fs;
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_symlink(back, "d/back/out", "../x")), escapes("../x"));
  let looped = mem_symlink(mem_symlink(fixture, "a", "b").fs, "b", "a").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_kind(looped, "a"), Symlink);
  assert_eq(mem_resolved_kind(looped, "a"), Missing);
  assert_eq(mem_read(looped, "a"), None)
}

test "a symlink whose target is outside the root fails when read or written, but can still be inspected, renamed over and removed" {
  let fixture = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "plain", body: b"p" }]), "d").fs;
  let moved = mem_rename(mem_symlink(fixture, "d/gone", "../out/gone").fs, "d/gone", "gone");
  assert(moved.ok);
  let tree = moved.fs;
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_read(tree, "gone")), escapes("gone"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_write(tree, "gone", b"x")), escapes("gone"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_append(tree, "gone", b"x")), escapes("gone"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_open(tree, "gone", ToWrite)), escapes("gone"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_copy(tree, "plain", "gone")), escapes("gone"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_create_dir(tree, "gone/below")), escapes("gone/below"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_kind(tree, "gone/below")), escapes("gone/below"));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(tree, "gone"), Symlink);
  assert_eq(mem_read_link(tree, "gone"), Some("../out/gone"));
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "gone", ToCreate).file, Err(Exists));
  assert(mem_rename(tree, "plain", "gone").ok);
  let removed = mem_remove(tree, "gone");
  assert(removed.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_kind(removed.fs, "gone"), Missing)
}

test "a walk is depth first in name order and lists a link without following it" {
  let fixture = mem_of([
    { path: "a/b.txt", body: b"b" },
    { path: "a/z/y.txt", body: b"y" },
    { path: "a-c.txt", body: b"c" },
  ]);
  let linked = mem_symlink(fixture, "link", "a").fs;
  assert_eq(
    mem_walk(linked, ""),
    Some([
      { path: "a", kind: Dir },
      { path: "a/b.txt", kind: File },
      { path: "a/z", kind: Dir },
      { path: "a/z/y.txt", kind: File },
      { path: "a-c.txt", kind: File },
      { path: "link", kind: Symlink },
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(
    mem_walk(linked, "./a"),
    Some([
      { path: "a/b.txt", kind: File },
      { path: "a/z", kind: Dir },
      { path: "a/z/y.txt", kind: File },
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(mem_walk(linked, "a/b.txt"), None);
  assert_eq(mem_walk(linked, "absent"), None)
}

test "a stamp sets the modification time a later read returns" {
  let fixture = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"x" }]), "d").fs;
  let finish_file_copy = mem_set_modified(
    mem_set_modified(fixture, "a.txt", 1000000000000).fs,
    "d",
    7,
  ).fs;
  assert_eq(mem_modified(finish_file_copy, "a.txt"), Some(1000000000000));
  assert_eq(mem_modified(finish_file_copy, "d"), Some(7));
  assert(!mem_set_modified(fixture, "absent", 1).ok)
}

test "a directory moves with everything under it, and never into itself" {
  let fixture = mem_of([{ path: "a/b/c.txt", body: b"c" }, { path: "x.txt", body: b"x" }]);
  let moved = mem_rename(fixture, "a", "z");
  assert(moved.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(moved.fs, "z/b/c.txt"), Some(b"c"));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(moved.fs, "a"), Missing);
  assert(!mem_rename(fixture, "a", "a/b/inside").ok);
  assert(!mem_rename(fixture, "x.txt", "a").ok);
  let replaced = mem_rename(mem_write(fixture, "y.txt", b"y").fs, "x.txt", "y.txt");
  assert(replaced.ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read(replaced.fs, "y.txt"), Some(b"x"))
}

test "`temp_dir`, `copy`, `walk` and `remove_tree` through a handler behave like the in-memory filesystem's own operations" {
  handle {
    let made = match fs.temp_dir[work]("", "run-") { Some(p) -> p, None -> panic("a directory") };
    assert(fs.copy[work]("src/m.ply", made ++ "/m.ply"));
    assert_eq(fs.walk[work](made), Some([{ path: made ++ "/m.ply", kind: File }]));
    assert(fs.remove_tree[work](made));
    assert(!fs.exists[work](made))
  } with {
    var t = mem_of([{ path: "src/m.ply", body: b"m" }]),
    fs.temp_dir[work](dir, prefix) -> {
      let m = mem_temp_dir(t, dir, prefix);
      t = m.fs;
      m.path
    },
    fs.copy[work](from, to) -> {
      let w = mem_copy(t, from, to);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.walk[work](p) -> mem_walk(t, p),
    fs.remove_tree[work](p) -> {
      let w = mem_remove_tree(t, p);
      t = w.fs;
      w.ok
    },
    fs.exists[work](p) -> mem_exists(t, p),
  }
}

fn link_to_copy() -> Option<String> / {fs.read[disk], fs.write[disk]} = {
  let dir = fs.temp_dir[disk]("", "t-")?;
  if fs.copy[disk]("a.txt", dir ++ "/b.txt") && fs.symlink[disk](dir ++ "/l", "b.txt") {
    fs.read_link[disk](dir ++ "/l")
  } else { None }
}

test "code that may call any filesystem operation runs against an in-memory filesystem with `on_mem`" {
  let over = on_mem(mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"x" }]), || link_to_copy());
  assert_eq(over.value, Some("b.txt"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "t-2/b.txt"), Some(b"x"))
}

fn descriptor_of(o: OpenResult) -> Int / {abort.raise} =
  match o.file { Ok(file) -> file, Err(_) -> panic("the file failed to open") }

test "a descriptor reads what comes next a chunk at a time, and an empty chunk is its file's end" {
  let o = mem_open(mem_of([{ path: "log", body: b"abcdefg" }]), "log", ToRead);
  let file = descriptor_of(o);
  let first = mem_read_chunk(o.fs, file, 3);
  let short = mem_read_chunk(mem_short_reads(first.fs, 2), file, 100);
  let rest = mem_read_chunk(mem_short_reads(short.fs, 0), file, 100);
  let ended = mem_read_chunk(rest.fs, file, 100);
  assert_eq([first.bytes, short.bytes, rest.bytes, ended.bytes], [
    Some(b"abc"),
    Some(b"de"),
    Some(b"fg"),
    Some(b""),
  ]);
  assert(!mem_write_chunk(ended.fs, file, b"x").ok);
  let closed = mem_close(ended.fs, file);
  assert(closed.ok);
  assert(!mem_close(closed.fs, file).ok);
  assert_eq(mem_read_chunk(closed.fs, file, 1).bytes, None)
}

test "a descriptor opened to write truncates its file, and one opened to append writes at its end" {
  let w = mem_open(mem_of([{ path: "log", body: b"old" }]), "log", ToWrite);
  let file = descriptor_of(w);
  let written = mem_write_chunk(mem_write_chunk(w.fs, file, b"ab").fs, file, b"cd").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_read(written, "log"), Some(b"abcd"));
  assert_eq(mem_read_chunk(written, file, 1).bytes, None);
  let a = mem_open(mem_close(written, file).fs, "log", ToAppend);
  assert(a.file != w.file);
  let appended = mem_write_chunk(a.fs, descriptor_of(a), b"ef").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_read(appended, "log"), Some(b"abcdef"));
  let made = mem_open(appended, "new", ToAppend);
  assert_eq(mem_read(mem_write_chunk(made.fs, descriptor_of(made), b"x").fs, "new"), Some(b"x"))
}

test "an open reports why a file did not open" {
  let none = access(false, false, false);
  let fixture = mem_set_mode(mem_of([{ path: "d/f", body: b"x" }]), "d/f", {
    owner: none,
    group: none,
    other: none,
  }).fs;
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "absent", ToRead).file, Err(NotFound));
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "d", ToRead).file, Err(NotAFile));
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "d", ToWrite).file, Err(NotAFile));
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "no/f", ToWrite).file, Err(NotFound));
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "d/f", ToRead).file, Err(Denied));
  assert_eq(mem_open(fixture, "d/f", ToAppend).file, Err(Denied))
}

law "`mode_of_bits` is the inverse of `mode_bits` for a mode's nine permission bits" =
  inverse(mode_bits, mode_of_bits)

test "a mode's bits are the owner's, the group's and everyone else's, and no bit above them" {
  assert_eq(mode_bits(mode_of("rwxr-x--x")), 489);
  assert_eq(mode_of_bits(420), mode_of("rw-r--r--"));
  assert_eq(mode_of_bits(4096 + 420), mode_of("rw-r--r--"))
}

fn shape(found: Option<Stat>) -> String =
  match found {
    None -> "nothing",
    Some(s) ->
      (match s.kind { File -> "file", Dir -> "dir", Symlink -> "link", Missing -> "missing" })
        ++ " "
        ++ s.size.to_string()
        ++ " "
        ++ mode_text(s.mode)
        ++ " x"
        ++ s.links.to_string(),
  }

fn id_at(tree: MemFs, path: String) -> Int / {abort.raise} =
  match mem_stat(tree, path) { Some(found) -> found.id, None -> panic(path ++ " is not there") }

fn shapes(seen: Option<List<ScanEntry>>) -> List<String> =
  match seen {
    Some(entries) -> entries.map(|s: ScanEntry| s.path ++ ": " ++ shape(Some(s.stat))),
    None -> ["none"],
  }

test "`mem_stat` returns a path's kind, size, modification time, mode and link count, and describes a symlink itself" {
  let base = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "d/a.txt", body: b"abc" }]), "d/sub").fs;
  let tree = mem_symlink(mem_set_modified(base, "d/a.txt", 5).fs, "d/l", "a.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(tree, "d/a.txt")), "file 3 rw-r--r-- x1");
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(tree, "d")), "dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1");
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(tree, "d/l")), "link 0 rwxrwxrwx x1");
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(tree, "")), "dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1");
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(tree, "d/absent")), "nothing");
  assert_eq(
    match mem_stat(tree, "d/a.txt") { Some(found) -> Some(found.modified), None -> None },
    Some(Instant(5000000)),
  );
  assert(id_at(tree, "d/a.txt") != id_at(tree, "d/l"));
  assert(id_at(tree, "d/a.txt") != id_at(tree, "d/sub"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_stat(tree, "../d")), escapes("../d"))
}

test "`mem_scan` returns the `mem_stat` result for each entry under a directory, and descends into subdirectories only when asked" {
  let tree = mem_symlink(
    mem_of([
      { path: "a/b.txt", body: b"b" },
      { path: "a/z/y.txt", body: b"yy" },
      { path: "c.txt", body: b"" },
    ]),
    "link",
    "a",
  ).fs;
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(tree, "", true)), [
    "a: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "a/b.txt: file 1 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "a/z: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "a/z/y.txt: file 2 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "c.txt: file 0 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "link: link 0 rwxrwxrwx x1",
  ]);
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(tree, "", false)), [
    "a: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "c.txt: file 0 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "link: link 0 rwxrwxrwx x1",
  ]);
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(tree, "./a/", false)), [
    "a/b.txt: file 1 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "a/z: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
  ]);
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(tree, "c.txt", true)), ["none"]);
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(tree, "absent", true)), ["none"]);
  assert_eq(
    match mem_scan(tree, "", true) {
      Some(seen) -> Some(seen.map(|s: ScanEntry| { path: s.path, kind: s.stat.kind })),
      None -> None,
    },
    mem_walk(tree, ""),
  )
}

test "a file with two names is one file, until its last name is removed" {
  let made = mem_link(mem_of([{ path: "a.txt", body: b"one" }]), "d.txt", "a.txt");
  assert_eq(made.done, Ok(()));
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(made.fs, "a.txt")), "file 3 rw-r--r-- x2");
  assert_eq(id_at(made.fs, "a.txt"), id_at(made.fs, "d.txt"));
  let written = mem_write(made.fs, "d.txt", b"both").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_read(written, "a.txt"), Some(b"both"));
  assert_eq(mem_modified(written, "a.txt"), mem_modified(written, "d.txt"));
  assert_eq(mem_copy(written, "a.txt", "d.txt"), { fs: written, ok: true });
  assert_eq(mem_copy(written, "a.txt", "a.txt"), { fs: written, ok: true });
  let closed = mem_set_mode(written, "a.txt", mode_of("rw-------")).fs;
  assert_eq(mem_mode(closed, "d.txt"), Some(mode_of("rw-------")));
  let renamed = mem_rename(closed, "a.txt", "b.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_read(mem_append(renamed, "b.txt", b"!").fs, "d.txt"), Some(b"both!"));
  let onto_itself = mem_rename(renamed, "b.txt", "d.txt");
  assert(onto_itself.ok);
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(onto_itself.fs, "")), Some(["b.txt", "d.txt"]));
  let left = mem_remove(renamed, "b.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(left, "d.txt")), "file 4 rw------- x1");
  assert_eq(mem_read(left, "d.txt"), Some(b"both"));
  let third = mem_link(mem_link(renamed, "c.txt", "b.txt").fs, "e.txt", "d.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(shape(mem_stat(mem_remove(third, "c.txt").fs, "e.txt")), "file 4 rw------- x3")
}

test "`mem_link` adds a second name only for an existing file, and fails when the new name exists or its directory is missing" {
  let tree = mem_symlink(mem_of([{ path: "d/a.txt", body: b"x" }]), "l", "d/a.txt").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "n", "absent").done, Err(NotFound));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "n", "d").done, Err(NotAFile));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "n", "l").done, Err(NotAFile));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "l", "d/a.txt").done, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "d/a.txt", "d/a.txt").done, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "no/n", "d/a.txt").done, Err(NotFound));
  assert_eq(mem_link(tree, "d/a.txt/n", "d/a.txt").done, Err(NotADirectory));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_link(tree, "../n", "d/a.txt")), escapes("../n"));
  assert_eq(raised(|| mem_link(tree, "n", "/d/a.txt")), escapes("/d/a.txt"))
}

test "a file created with `ToCreate` is readable and writable by its owner only, and opening fails when the path already exists" {
  let tree = mem_symlink(mem_of([{ path: "d/a.txt", body: b"x" }]), "d/l", "nowhere").fs;
  let made = mem_open(tree, "d/new", ToCreate);
  let written = mem_write_chunk(made.fs, descriptor_of(made), b"mine").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_read(written, "d/new"), Some(b"mine"));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(written, "d/new"), Some(mode_of("rw-------")));
  assert_eq(mem_open(written, "d/new", ToCreate).file, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "d/a.txt", ToCreate).file, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "d", ToCreate).file, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "d/l", ToCreate).file, Err(Exists));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(tree, "d/nowhere"), Missing);
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "no/new", ToCreate).file, Err(NotFound));
  assert_eq(mem_open(tree, "d/a.txt/new", ToCreate).file, Err(NotADirectory))
}

test "`mem_space` returns the configured disk space for any existing path, and `None` for a missing path" {
  let room = { total: 100, free: 40, available: 30, inodes: 10, inodes_free: 4 };
  let tree = mem_with_space(mem_of([{ path: "a", body: b"" }]), room);
  assert_eq(mem_space(tree, ""), Some(room));
  assert_eq(mem_space(tree, "a"), Some(room));
  assert_eq(mem_space(tree, "absent"), None);
  assert_eq(
    match mem_space(mem_empty(), "") { Some(found) -> found.available > 0, None -> false },
    true,
  )
}

test "`mem_rename` preserves a file's modification time, and a renamed directory's files move with it" {
  let tree = mem_set_modified(mem_of([{ path: "d/a", body: b"x" }]), "d/a", 7).fs;
  assert_eq(mem_modified(mem_rename(tree, "d/a", "d/b").fs, "d/b"), Some(7));
  let moved = mem_rename(tree, "d", "e").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_modified(moved, "e/a"), Some(7));
  assert_eq(id_at(moved, "e/a"), id_at(tree, "d/a"));
  assert_eq(id_at(moved, "e"), id_at(tree, "d"))
}

test "after a simulated power loss, a file contains its last synced bytes, and a created, removed or renamed file keeps its old name unless the parent directory was synced" {
  let base = mem_sync_all(mem_of([{ path: "d/old", body: b"old" }]));
  let after = |tree: MemFs, path: String| mem_read(mem_crashed(tree), path);
  assert_eq(after(base, "d/old"), Some(b"old"));
  let rewritten = mem_write(base, "d/old", b"new!").fs;
  assert_eq(after(rewritten, "d/old"), Some(b"old"));
  assert_eq(after(mem_sync(rewritten, "d/old").fs, "d/old"), Some(b"new!"));
  let made = mem_write(base, "d/fresh", b"data").fs;
  assert_eq(mem_kind(mem_crashed(made), "d/fresh"), Missing);
  assert_eq(after(mem_sync(made, "d").fs, "d/fresh"), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(after(mem_sync(made, "d/fresh").fs, "d/fresh"), None);
  assert_eq(after(mem_sync(mem_sync(made, "d/fresh").fs, "d").fs, "d/fresh"), Some(b"data"));
  let removed = mem_remove(base, "d/old").fs;
  assert_eq(after(removed, "d/old"), Some(b"old"));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(mem_crashed(mem_sync(removed, "d").fs), "d/old"), Missing);
  let renamed = mem_rename(base, "d/old", "d/young").fs;
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(mem_crashed(renamed), "d")), Some(["old"]));
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(mem_crashed(mem_sync(renamed, "d").fs), "d")), Some(["young"]));
  assert_eq(after(mem_sync(renamed, "d").fs, "d/young"), Some(b"old"));
  assert_eq(after(mem_rename(base, "d", "e").fs, "e/old"), Some(b"old"));
  assert(!mem_sync(base, "d/absent").ok);
  let opened = mem_open(base, "d/old", ToRead);
  assert_eq(mem_crashed(mem_lock(opened.fs, "d/lock").fs).opened.len(), 0)
}

test "`on_mem_counted` returns how many filesystem operations ran, counting a read the same as a write" {
  let ran = on_mem_counted[d](mem_of([{ path: "a", body: b"1" }]), || {
    fs.read_file[d]("a");
    fs.exists[d]("b");
    fs.write_file[d]("b", b"2")
  });
  assert(ran.value);
  assert_eq(ran.operations, 3);
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(ran.tree, "")), Some(["a", "b"]))
}

test "a disk that fails after a number of writes changes nothing from then on, and reports failure" {
  let over = on_mem_failing[d](mem_empty(), 2, ||
    [
      fs.write_file[d]("a", b"1"),
      fs.create_dir[d]("x"),
      fs.write_file[d]("b", b"2"),
      fs.remove[d]("a"),
    ]);
  assert_eq(over.value, [true, true, false, false]);
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(over.tree, "")), Some(["a", "x"]));
  let gone = on_mem_failing[d](mem_of([{ path: "a", body: b"1" }]), 0, || {
    let made = fs.open[d]("new", ToCreate);
    let named = fs.link[d]("b", "a");
    let read = match fs.open[d]("a", ToRead) {
      Ok(file) -> {
        let bytes = fs.read_chunk[d](file, 8);
        if fs.close[d](file) { bytes } else { None }
      },
      Err(_) -> None,
    };
    (made, named, read, fs.sync[d]("a"), fs.append[d]("a", b"x"), fs.temp_dir[d]("", "t-"))
  });
  assert_eq(gone.value, (Err(failed()), Err(failed()), Some(b"1"), false, None, None));
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(gone.tree, "")), Some(["a"]));
  assert_eq(gone.tree.opened.len(), 0)
}

fn counted<a | e>(go: () -> a / {entropy.next, entropy.below | e}) -> a / {abort.raise | e} =
  with_rand(rand(0), go).value

fn replaced(
  tree: MemFs,
  writes: Int,
  body: Bytes,
  durability: Durability,
) -> MemResult<Result<Unit, (String, FileError)>> / {abort.raise} =
  on_mem_failing[d](tree, writes, ||
    counted(|| {
      let wrote: Result<Unit, (String, FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
        write_atomic[d]("d/cfg", body, durability)
      };
      wrote
    }))

fn temporaries(tree: MemFs, dir: String) -> List<String> / {abort.raise} =
  match listed_names(mem_list(tree, dir)) {
    Some(names) -> names.filter(|name: String| name.starts_with(".tmp-")),
    None -> [],
  }

test "`write_atomic` replaces a file's contents and the new file keeps the old file's mode, and no temporary file is left behind" {
  let base = mem_set_mode(
    mem_of([{ path: "d/cfg", body: b"old" }]),
    "d/cfg",
    mode_of("rw-r-----"),
  ).fs;
  let over = on_mem[d](base, ||
    counted(|| {
      write_atomic[d]("d/cfg", b"new", Durable);
      write_atomic[d]("d/fresh", b"f", Fast);
      write_atomic[d]("d/tool", b"t", Fast, Some(mode_of("rwx------")))
    }));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "d/cfg"), Some(b"new"));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(over.tree, "d/cfg"), Some(mode_of("rw-r-----")));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(over.tree, "d/fresh"), Some(mode_of("rw-r--r--")));
  assert_eq(mem_mode(over.tree, "d/tool"), Some(mode_of("rwx------")));
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(over.tree, "d")), Some(["cfg", "fresh", "tool"]));
  assert_eq(over.tree.opened.len(), 0)
}

test "`write_atomic` replaces a symlink or a hard-linked name with a new file, leaving the link target and other names unchanged" {
  let linked = mem_symlink(mem_of([{ path: "d/real", body: b"real" }]), "d/cfg", "real").fs;
  let named = mem_link(linked, "d/twin", "d/real").fs;
  let over = on_mem[d](named, ||
    counted(|| {
      write_atomic[d]("d/cfg", b"new", Fast);
      write_atomic[d]("d/twin", b"apart", Fast)
    }));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(over.tree, "d/cfg"), File);
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "d/cfg"), Some(b"new"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "d/twin"), Some(b"apart"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "d/real"), Some(b"real"))
}

fn atomic_write_fails<[l]>(
  path: String,
) -> Unit / {fs.open[l], fs.space[l], fs.sync[l], fs.close[l], fs.stat[l], fs.set_mode[l], fs.rename[l], fs.remove[l], files.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  handle { write_atomic[l](path, b"new", Durable) } with { fs.write_chunk[l](file, body) -> false }

test "an atomic write that cannot be made reports where it stopped and why, and leaves the path unchanged" {
  let base = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "d/cfg", body: b"old" }]), "d/dir").fs;
  let tried = |tree: MemFs, path: String|
    on_mem[d](tree, ||
      counted(|| {
        let wrote: Result<Unit, (String, FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
          write_atomic[d](path, b"new", Durable)
        };
        match wrote { Ok(_) -> None, Err((_, reason)) -> Some(reason) }
      }));
  assert_eq(tried(base, "no/cfg").value, Some(NotFound));
  assert_eq(tried(base, "d/cfg/under").value, Some(NotADirectory));
  let onto_dir = tried(base, "d/dir");
  assert_eq(onto_dir.value, Some(Other(0, "rename")));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(onto_dir.tree, "d/dir"), Dir);
  assert_eq(temporaries(onto_dir.tree, "d"), []);
  let roomy = mem_empty().space;
  let full = |room: Int|
    on_mem[d](mem_with_space(base, { ..roomy, available: room }), ||
      counted(|| {
        let wrote: Result<Unit, (String, FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
          atomic_write_fails[d]("d/cfg")
        };
        match wrote { Ok(_) -> None, Err((_, reason)) -> Some(reason) }
      }));
  assert_eq(full(2).value, Some(NoSpace));
  assert_eq(full(3).value, Some(Other(0, "write_chunk")));
  assert_eq(mem_read(full(2).tree, "d/cfg"), Some(b"old"));
  assert_eq(temporaries(full(2).tree, "d"), [])
}

test "an atomic write that fails after {writes} steps leaves the file with either its old or its new contents, including after a simulated power loss" for writes: Int in [
    0,
    1,
    2,
    3,
    4,
    5,
    6,
    7,
  ] {
  let base = mem_sync_all(mem_of([{ path: "d/cfg", body: b"old" }]));
  let whole = |found: Option<Bytes>| found == Some(b"old") || found == Some(b"new!");
  let over = replaced(base, writes, b"new!", Durable);
  assert(whole(mem_read(over.tree, "d/cfg")));
  assert(whole(mem_read(mem_crashed(over.tree), "d/cfg")));
  assert_eq(over.value == Ok(()), writes >= 6);
  if writes >= 6 { assert_eq(mem_read(mem_crashed(over.tree), "d/cfg"), Some(b"new!")) }
  else { assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "d/cfg") == Some(b"new!"), writes == 5) };
  let fast = replaced(base, writes, b"new!", Fast);
  assert(whole(mem_read(fast.tree, "d/cfg")));
  assert_eq(fast.value == Ok(()), writes >= 4)
}

test "after a simulated power loss, a `Fast` write may lose its new bytes, but a `Durable` write still has them" {
  let base = mem_sync_all(mem_of([{ path: "d/cfg", body: b"old" }]));
  let fast = replaced(base, 100, b"new!", Fast).tree;
  let durable = replaced(base, 100, b"new!", Durable).tree;
  assert_eq(mem_read(mem_crashed(fast), "d/cfg"), Some(b"old"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(mem_crashed(mem_sync(fast, "d").fs), "d/cfg"), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(mem_read(mem_crashed(durable), "d/cfg"), Some(b"new!"))
}

test "`write_atomic` succeeds after an earlier failed write left a temporary file, and adds no new temporary file" {
  let base = mem_sync_all(mem_of([{ path: "d/cfg", body: b"old" }]));
  let died = replaced(base, 2, b"half", Durable).tree;
  assert_eq(temporaries(died, "d").len(), 1);
  let again = replaced(died, 100, b"new!", Durable);
  assert_eq(again.value, Ok(()));
  assert_eq(mem_read(again.tree, "d/cfg"), Some(b"new!"));
  assert_eq(temporaries(again.tree, "d"), temporaries(died, "d"))
}

fn within(entries: Int, bytes: Int) -> CopyOptions =
  { existing: Reject, outside_link: RejectLink, entries: entries, bytes: bytes }

fn sample_tree() -> MemFs / {abort.raise} = {
  let files = mem_of([{ path: "src/a.txt", body: b"a" }, { path: "src/sub/b.txt", body: b"bb" }]);
  let closed = mem_set_mode(
    mem_create_dir(files, "src/empty").fs,
    "src/a.txt",
    mode_of("rw-------"),
  ).fs;
  let dated = mem_set_modified(mem_set_modified(closed, "src/a.txt", 5).fs, "src/sub", 9).fs;
  mem_link(mem_symlink(dated, "src/l", "sub/b.txt").fs, "src/sub/c.txt", "src/a.txt").fs
}

fn counting(n: Int, _told: Progress) -> Iter<Int, Int> = Continue(n + 1)

fn copy_tried(
  tree: MemFs,
  from: String,
  to: String,
  how: CopyOptions,
) -> MemResult<Result<CopyResult<Int>, (String, FileError)>> / {abort.raise} =
  on_mem[d](tree, || {
    let copied: Result<CopyResult<Int>, (String, FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
      copy_tree[d](from, to, how, 0, counting)
    };
    copied
  })

test "a tree is copied with its bytes, modes and times, a link as a link and two names as one file" {
  let over = copy_tried(sample_tree(), "src", "out/copy", within(100, 1000));
  assert_eq(over.value, Ok({ value: 7, entries: 7, bytes: 4, skipped: [], stopped: false }));
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(over.tree, "out/copy", true)), [
    "out/copy/a.txt: file 1 rw------- x2",
    "out/copy/empty: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "out/copy/l: link 0 rwxrwxrwx x1",
    "out/copy/sub: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "out/copy/sub/b.txt: file 2 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "out/copy/sub/c.txt: file 1 rw------- x2",
  ]);
  assert_eq(mem_read_link(over.tree, "out/copy/l"), Some("sub/b.txt"));
  assert_eq(id_at(over.tree, "out/copy/a.txt"), id_at(over.tree, "out/copy/sub/c.txt"));
  assert(id_at(over.tree, "out/copy/a.txt") != id_at(over.tree, "src/a.txt"));
  assert_eq(mem_modified(over.tree, "out/copy/a.txt"), Some(5));
  assert_eq(mem_modified(over.tree, "out/copy/sub"), Some(9));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "out/copy/sub/b.txt"), Some(b"bb"));
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(over.tree, "src", true)), shapes(mem_scan(sample_tree(), "src", true)))
}

test "`copy_tree` to an existing destination fails with `Reject`, merges into it with `Merge`, and deletes its old contents first with `Replace`" {
  let stood = mem_of([
    { path: "src/a.txt", body: b"new" },
    { path: "src/sub/b.txt", body: b"b" },
    { path: "out/a.txt", body: b"old" },
    { path: "out/kept.txt", body: b"kept" },
  ]);
  let linked = mem_symlink(stood, "out/sub", "kept.txt").fs;
  let how = |existing: Existing| { ..within(100, 1000), existing: existing };
  let rejected = copy_tried(linked, "src", "out", how(Reject));
  assert_eq(rejected.value, Err(("out", Exists)));
  assert_eq(mem_read(rejected.tree, "out/a.txt"), Some(b"old"));
  let merged = copy_tried(linked, "src", "out", how(Merge)).tree;
  assert_eq(mem_read(merged, "out/a.txt"), Some(b"new"));
  assert_eq(mem_read(merged, "out/kept.txt"), Some(b"kept"));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(merged, "out/sub"), Dir);
  assert_eq(mem_read(merged, "out/sub/b.txt"), Some(b"b"));
  let anew = copy_tried(linked, "src", "out", how(Replace)).tree;
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(anew, "out")), Some(["a.txt", "sub"]));
  let over_tree = mem_create_dir(mem_of([{ path: "src/a", body: b"file" }]), "out/a/deep").fs;
  assert_eq(copy_tried(over_tree, "src", "out", how(Merge)).value, Err(("out/a", NotAFile)));
  assert_eq(copy_tried(linked, "src", "", how(Replace)).value, Err(("", Other(0, "remove_tree"))))
}

test "`copy_tree` fails before copying when over the entry or byte limit, stops when the callback returns `Stop`, and fails for a missing source" {
  assert_eq(
    copy_tried(sample_tree(), "src", "out", within(6, 1000)).value,
    Err(("src", LimitExceeded)),
  );
  let over_bytes = copy_tried(sample_tree(), "src", "out", within(100, 3));
  assert_eq(over_bytes.value, Err(("src", LimitExceeded)));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(over_bytes.tree, "out"), Missing);
  assert_eq(
    copy_tried(sample_tree(), "absent", "out", within(100, 1000)).value,
    Err(("absent", NotFound)),
  );
  let stopped = on_mem[d](sample_tree(), ||
    copy_tree[d](
      "src",
      "out",
      within(100, 1000),
      [],
      |seen: List<String>, apply_step: Progress|
        if seen.len() < 1 { Continue(seen.push(apply_step.path)) }
        else { Stop(seen.push(apply_step.path)) },
    ));
  assert_eq(stopped.value.value, ["out", "out/a.txt"]);
  assert_eq(stopped.value.stopped, true);
  assert_eq(listed_names(mem_list(stopped.tree, "out")), Some(["a.txt"]));
  let one = copy_tried(sample_tree(), "src/sub/b.txt", "b.copy", within(1, 2));
  assert_eq(one.value, Ok({ value: 1, entries: 1, bytes: 2, skipped: [], stopped: false }));
  assert_eq(mem_read(one.tree, "b.copy"), Some(b"bb"))
}

test "a link that would point out of the root it is copied to is rejected, or left out and reported" {
  let tree = mem_symlink(
    mem_of([{ path: "src/a", body: b"a" }, { path: "sib", body: b"s" }]),
    "src/up",
    "../sib",
  ).fs;
  let how = |outside: OutsideLink|
    { existing: Merge, outside_link: outside, entries: 100, bytes: 1000 };
  assert_eq(copy_tried(tree, "src", "", how(RejectLink)).value, Err(("up", CrossesRoot)));
  let skipped = copy_tried(tree, "src", "", how(SkipLink));
  assert_eq(skipped.value, Ok({ value: 2, entries: 2, bytes: 1, skipped: ["up"], stopped: false }));
  assert_eq(mem_kind(skipped.tree, "up"), Missing);
  let deeper = copy_tried(tree, "src", "x/y", how(RejectLink)).tree;
  assert_eq(mem_read_link(deeper, "x/y/up"), Some("../sib"))
}

test "a tree is copied between two roots a chunk at a time, whatever short reads its files give" {
  let over = on_mem[from](mem_short_reads(sample_tree(), 1), ||
    on_mem[to](mem_empty(), ||
      copy_tree_across[from, to]("src", "copy", within(100, 1000), 0, counting)));
  assert_eq(over.value.value, { value: 7, entries: 7, bytes: 4, skipped: [], stopped: false });
  assert_eq(shapes(mem_scan(over.value.tree, "copy", true)), [
    "copy/a.txt: file 1 rw------- x2",
    "copy/empty: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "copy/l: link 0 rwxrwxrwx x1",
    "copy/sub: dir 0 rwxr-xr-x x1",
    "copy/sub/b.txt: file 2 rw-r--r-- x1",
    "copy/sub/c.txt: file 1 rw------- x2",
  ]);
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.value.tree, "copy/sub/b.txt"), Some(b"bb"));
  assert_eq(mem_modified(over.value.tree, "copy/a.txt"), Some(5));
  assert_eq(over.tree.opened.len(), 0);
  assert_eq(over.value.tree.opened.len(), 0)
}

fn seen_in(tree: MemFs) -> List<ScanEntry> / {abort.raise} =
  match mem_scan(tree, "", true) {
    Some(seen) -> seen,
    None -> panic("the scan returned None because the root is not a directory"),
  }

test "`changes` reports each difference between two directory snapshots once, as removed, renamed, created or modified" {
  let before = mem_of([
    { path: "a.txt", body: b"1" },
    { path: "b.txt", body: b"2" },
    { path: "d/x.txt", body: b"x" },
    { path: "d/y.txt", body: b"y" },
    { path: "gone.txt", body: b"g" },
    { path: "saved.txt", body: b"draft" },
  ]);
  let saved = mem_rename(mem_write(before, "saved.tmp", b"final").fs, "saved.tmp", "saved.txt").fs;
  let after = mem_write(
    mem_rename(
      mem_write(
        mem_rename(mem_remove(saved, "gone.txt").fs, "a.txt", "z.txt").fs,
        "b.txt",
        b"22",
      ).fs,
      "d",
      "e",
    ).fs,
    "e/new.txt",
    b"n",
  ).fs;
  assert_eq(changes(seen_in(before), seen_in(after)), [
    Removed("gone.txt"),
    Renamed("a.txt", "z.txt"),
    Renamed("d", "e"),
    Created("e/new.txt"),
    Modified("b.txt"),
    Modified("saved.txt"),
  ]);
  assert_eq(changes(seen_in(before), seen_in(before)), []);
  let closed = mem_set_mode(before, "d", mode_of("rwx------")).fs;
  assert_eq(changes(seen_in(before), seen_in(closed)), [Modified("d")]);
  let dated = mem_set_modified(before, "a.txt", 99).fs;
  assert_eq(changes(seen_in(before), seen_in(dated)), [Modified("a.txt")]);
  let moved_and_changed = mem_write(mem_rename(before, "a.txt", "z.txt").fs, "z.txt", b"11").fs;
  assert_eq(changes(seen_in(before), seen_in(moved_and_changed)), [
    Removed("a.txt"),
    Created("z.txt"),
  ])
}

test "each `poll` of a watch returns the changes since the previous poll, and never reports a change twice" {
  let over = on_mem[w](mem_of([{ path: "d/a.txt", body: b"a" }]), || {
    let quiet = poll[w](watch[w]("d", true, 10));
    fs.write_file[w]("d/n.txt", b"n");
    fs.write_file[w]("elsewhere.txt", b"e");
    let first = poll[w](quiet.watch);
    fs.remove[w]("d/a.txt");
    fs.create_dir[w]("d/sub/deep");
    let second = poll[w](first.watch);
    let above = watch[w]("d", false, 10);
    let shallow = poll[w]({ ..above, seen: quiet.watch.seen });
    [quiet.changes, first.changes, second.changes, poll[w](second.watch).changes, shallow.changes]
  });
  assert_eq(over.value, [
    [],
    [Created("d/n.txt")],
    [Removed("d/a.txt"), Created("d/sub"), Created("d/sub/deep")],
    [],
    [Removed("d/a.txt"), Created("d/n.txt"), Created("d/sub")],
  ])
}

test "a watch that lost track asks for a rescan, and never mistakes an unreadable directory for an empty one" {
  let over = on_mem[w](
    mem_of([{ path: "d/a.txt", body: b"a" }, { path: "d/b.txt", body: b"b" }]),
    || {
      let count_operation = watch[w]("d", true, 1);
      fs.write_file[w]("d/c.txt", b"c");
      fs.write_file[w]("d/e.txt", b"e");
      let flooded = poll[w](count_operation);
      fs.remove_tree[w]("d");
      let lost = poll[w](flooded.watch);
      let still = poll[w](lost.watch);
      fs.create_dir[w]("d");
      let back = poll[w](still.watch);
      fs.write_file[w]("d/f.txt", b"f");
      [flooded.changes, lost.changes, still.changes, back.changes, poll[w](back.watch).changes]
    },
  );
  assert_eq(over.value, [[Rescan], [Rescan], [], [Rescan], [Created("d/f.txt")]]);
  let unread = on_mem[w](mem_empty(), || poll[w](watch[w]("absent", true, 10)));
  assert_eq(unread.value.changes, []);
  assert_eq(unread.value.watch.seen, None)
}

fn awaited<[l] | e>(
  looks: Int,
  during: (Int) -> Unit / e,
) -> { changes: List<Change>, slept: Int } / {fs.scan[l], abort.raise | e} =
  handle {
    await_changes[l](watch[l]("", true, 10), millis(50), looks)
  } with {
    var slept = 0,
    time.sleep_ms(_) -> {
      let n = slept;
      slept = n + 1;
      during(n)
    },
    return found -> { changes: found.changes, slept: slept },
  }

test "`awaited` returns a burst of writes as one set of changes, after a poll finds the directory unchanged" {
  let over = on_mem[w](mem_of([{ path: "old.txt", body: b"o" }]), ||
    awaited[w](10, |n: Int|
      if n == 1 {
        fs.write_file[w]("a.txt", b"part");
        ()
      } else if n == 2 {
        fs.write_file[w]("a.txt", b"whole");
        fs.rename[w]("old.txt", "new.txt");
        ()
      } else { () }));
  assert_eq(over.value, { changes: [Renamed("old.txt", "new.txt"), Created("a.txt")], slept: 4 })
}

test "`awaited` returns no changes when nothing changes, or when a created file is removed again, before the allowed number of polls is used up" {
  let quiet = on_mem[w](mem_empty(), || awaited[w](3, |n: Int| ()));
  assert_eq(quiet.value, { changes: [], slept: 3 });
  let undone = on_mem[w](mem_empty(), ||
    awaited[w](4, |n: Int|
      if n == 0 {
        fs.write_file[w]("a.txt", b"a");
        ()
      } else if n == 1 {
        fs.remove[w]("a.txt");
        ()
      } else { () }));
  assert_eq(undone.value, { changes: [], slept: 4 });
  let unresolved = on_mem[w](mem_empty(), ||
    awaited[w](2, |n: Int| {
      fs.write_file[w]("a.txt", Bytes::of_string(n.to_string()));
      ()
    }));
  assert_eq(unresolved.value, { changes: [Created("a.txt")], slept: 2 })
}
